// Prose readings, keyed by Commons file title. Each says where the icon was made, what it
// shows, and what in it answers to Luke's verses — nothing the picture does not show, and
// nothing borrowed from another Gospel without saying whose it is.
module.exports={

// ---- chapter 1 ----

"File:051 Saint Luke Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg":
 "A Greek panel from the church of St Paraskevi at Langadas, undated by any signature but grouped by the church's own inventory with its companion evangelists, before the twentieth century. This is not a picture of these four verses — nothing in the Gospel names Luke as its own author, and no scene of Theophilus or of the many others who had taken the Gospel in hand is painted anywhere. It is the traditional author portrait Byzantine practice sets facing the opening of every Gospel: the writer himself, shown in the act the text implies rather than describes. Luke sits enthroned, haloed, a grey-bearded man in a dark tunic under a red mantle, his own name confirmed by Commons' own transcription of the small medallion at the upper left, ΛΟΥΚΑΣ. A second medallion at the upper right carries lettering this reading cannot confirm past a guess, so it does not name what it is meant to show. His right hand holds a slender quill above an open book propped on a stand before him, lines of Greek text lettered across both pages — legible as text, not as a transcribable sentence at this resolution, but its placement, opening the page just as the Gospel itself opens, is the icon's own way of showing forasmuch as many have taken in hand to set forth in order a declaration, a sentence Luke's own hand is shown committing to the page rather than merely holding. Beside his footstool at the lower right a winged ox lies couched, haloed at the throat — the Evangelist's own symbol from the Church's ancient reading of the four living creatures, given to Luke for the sacrificial, priestly note his Gospel opens on, at the altar of incense where Zacharias serves in the very next scene this reader shows.",

"File:Evangelismos Zachariou Dionysiou.jpg":
 "A fresco of 1547 from the Forerunner cycle in the katholikon of Dionysiou on Mount Athos, painted by Tzortzis Phouka and confirmed by Commons' own description: a fresco of the Annunciation to Zachariah. Its own title curls along the top of the recess: Ο ΕΥΑΓΓΕΛΙCΜΟC, the Annunciation — Zacharias's own, not the Theotokos's, which this reader shows three verses later at 1:26-38. Zacharias stands at the left, named beside his halo, ΖΑΧΑΡΙΑC: an old man, grey-bearded, in a red vestment, leaning toward the sanctuary with a censer swinging on its chain from his hand — his lot was to burn incense when he went into the temple of the Lord. Within a dark recessed apse, under a ribbed, reddish canopy raised on a row of slender columns — the ciborium over the altar of incense — a small winged angel, haloed, holds a thin staff: there appeared unto him an angel of the Lord. Luke sets him at a particular place, on the right side of the altar of incense, a detail the fresco does not resolve clearly enough at this distance to confirm or contradict, so this reading does not press it. At the lower right three figures wait together, one with both hands lifted in prayer: the whole multitude of the people were praying without at the time of incense, though the fresco gives them no more identity than that. A second haloed figure in a like red mantle is painted at the upper right, foreshortened along the curve of the vault; no legible inscription names him, and who he is meant to be, neither the fresco nor this reading can say.",

"File:017 Annunciation Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg":
 "A Greek panel of 1790 from the church of St Paraskevi at Langadas, dated by its own donor's prayer painted small at the foot: the servant of God, Peter, 1790. Gabriel comes from the left, wings raised, a green tunic under a red mantle, his hand lifted toward Mary: the angel came in unto her, and said, Hail, thou that art highly favoured, the Lord is with thee. Mary stands at the right in a red maphorion, both hands raised before her rather than one alone — the whole gesture of a woman startled rather than a single hand of blessing: when she saw him, she was troubled at his saying, and cast in her mind what manner of salutation this should be. Between them stands a green lectern with an open book, no text on it legible at this resolution; Church tradition reads her as caught at her reading when the angel comes, though Luke's own sentence says only that she was troubled, not what she had been doing. Behind both figures a walled city of red-roofed towers rises under a length of red cloth strung between them like a canopy — the standard architectural backdrop of a Byzantine Annunciation, no part of Luke's own account, which names only a city of Galilee, named Nazareth.",

"File:Annunciation Icon Sinai 12th century.jpg":
 "A Komnenian icon of the late twelfth century from St Catherine's Monastery on Sinai, its title worn but still legible in red across the top: Ο ΧΑΙΡΕΤΙCΜΟC, the Salutation — Gabriel's own first word to her, Hail. A small white dove, wings spread, descends on a narrow ray of light between the two figures, though the moment the icon paints is the greeting rather than the verse that names the dove's own work three sentences later: the Holy Ghost shall come upon thee, and the power of the Highest shall overshadow thee. Gabriel approaches from the left, one hand raised toward Mary; a thin herald's staff rests against his halo, its lower end lost against his shoulder. Mary is seated on an ornate throne at the right, before a tower-like building hung with a curtain in its own archway; her visible hand holds a slender rod against her shoulder, a skein of dark red thread looped over it — not a detail Luke's own text gives at all, but the Protoevangelium of James's account of her spinning the temple veil when the angel came, a tradition Byzantine icons paint as often as they paint anything from Luke's own sentence. Below both figures runs a narrow strip of animals and birds among reeds, a landscape convention rather than anything this passage narrates.",

"File:Visitation 04-18.jpg":
 "A fresco of 1502 from the Nativity cathedral at Ferapontov, painted by Dionisy, its own title in Church Slavonic letters across the sky, too worn to transcribe with confidence. Two haloed women stand cheek to cheek at the centre, under a length of rose-coloured cloth strung between the two buildings that flank them: Mary at the left, in a dark maroon mantle over blue; Elisabeth at the right, in green over red-brown. Mary's near hand rests on Elisabeth's shoulder, and Elisabeth's own hand comes up close beside Mary's at her waist, not at either woman's face — as soon as the voice of thy salutation sounded in mine ears, the babe leaped in my womb for joy, Elisabeth's own greeting a few verses later than the moment the embrace itself paints. Behind Elisabeth's building a pale rose curtain hangs in an open archway; behind Mary's, a domed canopy on slender columns. Neither structure is anything Luke's own text supplies. His own sentence for the moment gives only the house itself: and entered into the house of Zacharias, and saluted Elisabeth.",

"File:052 Nativity of Saint John the Baptist Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg":
 "A Greek panel from the church of St Paraskevi at Langadas, its own inscription confirming the subject even where the paint around it has gone: Η ΓΕΝΗCΗΣ ΤΟΥ Π[ΡΟΔΡΟΜΟΥ], the Nativity of the Forerunner. At the left Zacharias sits enthroned, haloed, bent over an open tablet on a stand, a boy attendant beside him: and he asked for a writing table, and wrote, saying, His name is John. Above and to the right a haloed, winged figure — an angel — leans in through an archway with a hand pointing into the scene; no inscription names him, and this reading does not guess what moment he is meant to mark. Toward the centre-right Elisabeth reclines on a red-covered bed, haloed, propped on pillows and holding the swaddled child, attended by three women who bring gifts and dress the bed: her neighbours and her cousins heard how the Lord had shewed great mercy upon her; and they rejoiced with her. At the lower left a fourth woman, seated, holds the naked infant on her lap while a second kneels before a large gold basin, testing the water — the washing of the newborn child, a convention the same church's own Nativity of Christ icon shows in exactly the same way, and no detail Luke's own eight verses give at all.",

// ---- chapter 2 ----

"File:031 Nativity of Jesus Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg":
 "A Greek panel from the church of St Paraskevi at Langadas, north of Thessaloniki, inscribed in the dark of the cave beside the Child: Η ΓΕΝΝΗΣΙΣ ΤΟΥ ΧΡΙΣΤΟΥ, the Nativity of Christ. The board has been badly hurt — a wide scar of lost paint falls from the top edge straight through the manger — and what is left is very nearly Luke's sentence and nothing else. The Child lies swaddled in gold-flecked bands in a manger built as a low stone chest, stepped at the foot the way an altar is stepped, and Luke gives the birth itself in one clause: and wrapped him in swaddling clothes, and laid him in a manger. The Theotokos kneels at the left and Joseph opposite her at the right, both with their hands crossed on the breast and both bowing to the manger rather than to each other; neither is doing anything, which is the painter's way of saying that nothing here was done by them. Behind Joseph an old man wrapped to the throat in a shaggy fleece stands with his head thrown back. Above the cave the panel carries the paragraph Luke writes next: on the hillside at the left an angel in orange leans down over two shepherds, one of them with a pipe held to his lips in both hands. The three riders coming over the hill at the right, and the eight-pointed star above the manger, are Matthew's — Luke has neither a star nor wise men. What he has instead is a decree from Caesar Augustus, a journey to the city of David, and no room for them in the inn.",

"File:12 Nativity of Jesus Icon in Assumption of Mary Church in Agios Vasileios.jpg":
 "A small panel from a village church of the Dormition at Agios Vasileios, and the most worn icon in this reader: the surface has been rubbed back so far that the haloes — thick rings of gold, dotted round the rim like jewellery — are often the only part of a figure still certain. Read what is legible and it is Luke's. At the centre stands a block of white masonry with a straight top and a moulded lip, and the Child lies along the top of it swaddled in rose-coloured bands. Whether the painter drew a trough with a far rim or a bare slab cannot be told — the surface is too far gone — but something is built there for him to be laid on, and and Luke's own clause for it is and wrapped him in swaddling clothes, and laid him in a manger. Two figures kneel to the trough with their hands crossed on the breast. The one on the right, in a rose maphorion under a jewelled halo, is the Theotokos; the old grey-bearded man in blue on the left is Joseph. Behind and between them stand others whose faces have gone altogether. A broad orange shaft comes down the middle of the board from the top edge to the manger. The upper register holds a small scene in each corner: at the right a figure in white raises his hand over a man who leans on a staff on the hillside, which is what Luke writes next — there were in the same country shepherds abiding in the field, keeping watch over their flock by night — and at the left a knot of small figures too far gone to name, of which nothing honest can be said but that they are there.",

"File:Nativity Icon Panagia Evraidos Church 19 Century.jpg":
 "A nineteenth-century Greek panel from the church of Panagia Evraidou, painted on a landscape ground of green hills and blue sky instead of gold, in the late Greek manner. It gives the shepherds as much of the board as the Holy Family, and it leaves out the one concrete thing Luke reports: there is no cave, no ox and ass, and no manger. The Child lies swaddled in white bands directly on the grass with thin gold rays spreading from him across it, and Joseph at the left and the Theotokos at the right each reach a hand toward him without touching him. Across the whole width of the panel runs a band of red letters; what is still legible in it is ΠΟΙΜΕΝ — the shepherds. Above the band, on the hillside, a haloed beardless youth in blue and red kneels and puts his hand out toward a shepherd who sits below him with his own hand raised and a long pipe across his lap: the painter has given the messenger a halo and no wings. Four angels lean over a bank of cloud along the top edge, two at either side of a triple shaft of red light, which is the multitude of the heavenly host praising God. Two pale beasts lie on the grass at the bottom of the panel below the Child's feet, the nearer one with a long muzzle and a drooping ear.",

"File:039 Circumcision of Jesus Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg":
 "A Greek panel from the church of St Paraskevi at Langadas, its own inscription confirming the subject: <Η> ΠΕΡΙΤΟΜΗ ΤΟΥ ΧΡΙΣΤΟΥ, the Circumcision of Christ. Under a domed ciborium raised on slender columns — the same architectural device this reader's Hypapante icon builds around the altar sixteen verses later — a priest in a tall gold mitre stands at a red-draped altar, a small blade held between two fingers, performing the rite Luke reports in a single clause: and when eight days were accomplished for the circumcising of the child, his name was called Jesus. The Child himself, haloed, lies on the altar before him. The Theotokos stands at the left, hands crossed on her breast; a haloed, grey-bearded elder — Joseph, by the same convention this reader's Nativity icons follow, though no lettering here names him — reaches a hand toward the Child from the altar's other side. Neither the priest nor the two unhaloed attendants flanking the scene, a bearded man at the far left and a young woman at the far right, is named by any lettering on the panel. Nothing here is Luke's beyond the rite itself and the naming that follows it, his name was called Jesus, which was so named of the angel before he was conceived in the womb — the panel gives no scene for the naming distinct from the circumcising, the same way Luke's own sentence folds both into one.",

"File:02 Circumcision of Jesus Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Adam.jpg":
 "A Greek panel of 1877 from the church of St Paraskevi at Adam, signed in its own lettering by its painter, Athanasios Vasilikos: χειρ Αθ. Β. 1877, \"the hand of Ath. B., 1877.\" Its inscription across the top confirms the subject twice over with the other Langadas panel, Η ΠΕΡΙΤΟΜΗ ΤΟΥ ΧΡΙΣΤΟΥ, and goes on to name two of its own figures where the other panel names none: Μ(ΗΤ)ΗΡ Θ(Ε)ΟΥ, Mother of God, beside the haloed woman at the right in a dark red maphorion, and ΙΩ(σήφ), Joseph, beside the haloed grey-bearded man beside her. Both stand apart from the rite itself, which a mitred priest performs at a canopied altar under a tall pointed hat, bent low over the Child laid on a red-and-gold cloth; a second, unhaloed figure in a like tall hat and gold brocade stands at the left with a hand raised, and a green-robed youth waits just behind the priest — neither named by the inscription, so neither is named here. Luke's own clause is the same one clause the Langadas panel paints: when eight days were accomplished for the circumcising of the child, his name was called Jesus. A separate roundel is painted into the lower right corner, its own haloed figure in green identified by the panel's own further lettering, Ο ΑΓΙΟΣ ΒΑΣΙΛΕΙΟΣ — St Basil the Great, whose own feast the Church keeps on the same day, 1 January, and whom Orthodox panels of this feast often pair beside it. He has no part in Luke's own eight verses; the panel says as much itself, by painting him in a frame of his own rather than inside the scene.",

"File:050 Presentation of Jesus at the Temple Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg":
 "A Greek panel from the church of St Paraskevi at Langadas, its own inscription confirming the feast even where the paint has failed around it: ΗΠΑΠΑν ΤΗ ΤΟΥ [ΧΡΙΣΤΟΥ] — the Hypapante, \"the Meeting,\" the Church's own name for this feast rather than a phrase of Luke's. Under a domed ciborium raised on a ring of columns, Symeon receives the Child at the centre, his hands covered by the fold of his own mantle in the reverence Byzantine icons give to anyone handling something holy — the man Luke names as just and devout, waiting for the consolation of Israel... it was revealed unto him by the Holy Ghost, that he should not see death, before he had seen the Lord's Christ. Mary stands at his left, both hands still reaching toward the Child she has just given up to him; Joseph is behind her, haloed, holding a pair of white doves in his hands — a pair of turtledoves, or two young pigeons, Luke's own detail for the offering the law required of the poor. At the right, a haloed woman holds a scroll: Anna, a prophetess, of the tribe of Aser, who Luke says coming in that instant gave thanks likewise unto the Lord, and spake of him to all them that looked for redemption in Jerusalem — the scroll is the icon's own way of showing a prophetess's own office, not a detail Luke's text itself describes her holding. All five figures the panel paints are Luke's own; nothing here is borrowed from outside this passage.",

// ---- chapter 3 ----

"File:St John the Baptist Late XVII - Early XVIII Cenury St Mary Blonichka Church, Ohris Icon Gallery.jpg":
 "A panel of about 1700 from the church of St Mary Blonichka at Ohrid. It is not a scene: it is the Forerunner as the Church paints him, standing full length with two great feathered wings folded up behind his shoulders. The wings are not a claim that he was an angel. The Greek word for a messenger is angelos, and Luke does put that word in the Lord's mouth about him — This is he, of whom it is written, Behold, I send my messenger before thy face, which shall prepare thy way before thee — but he says it four chapters after these verses, and the painter has taken it literally. He wears the shaggy hide of camel's hair over a blue-green himation, and a plain pale staff stands upright against his left side. The open scroll in his hand gives a sermon Luke never reports: ΜΕΤΑΝΟΕΙΤΕ ΗΓΓΙΚΕΝ ΓΑΡ Η ΒΑΣΙΛΕΙΑ ΤΩΝ ΟΥΡΑΝΩΝ — Repent ye: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand — which is Matthew's wording of the preaching. Luke's own summary is a single clause, preaching the baptism of repentance for the remission of sins, and then he stands aside and lets Isaiah speak for three verses. In the charger at the Forerunner's waist lies his own severed head with its eyes closed, painted while the living man stands above it, and that is why the panel belongs to the man and not to any one passage about him. Luke has the better claim to it even so: he alone tells the Forerunner's conception and his birth and his father's name, and he alone says where the years between went — the child grew, and waxed strong in spirit, and was in the deserts till the day of his shewing unto Israel.",

"File:Prodromos Didaskon Ioudaious Dionysiou.jpg":
 "A fresco of 1547 from the Forerunner cycle in the katholikon of Dionysiou on Mount Athos. The inscription runs across the top on either side of the halo: Ο ΠΡΟΔΡΟΜΟΣ ΔΙΔΑΣΚΩΝ ΤΟΥΣ ΙΟΥΔΑΙΟΥΣ, the Forerunner teaching the Jews. He stands barefoot in a wilderness of grey rock, the garment of hair under a green-gold himation, his right hand up in the gesture of speech and a tall staff ending in a small cross held against his left shoulder. What makes the fresco Luke's is that it paints his hearers in two kinds, and puts them on opposite sides of him. At his right a close press of five or six bearded men in soft caps and long robes, unarmed, leaning in over each other's shoulders: the multitude that came forth to be baptized of him, and the publicans who came with them. At his left, in scale corselets and lamellar skirts and helmets and greaves, with red cloaks over their shoulders, stand soldiers — five of them, the foremost cradling something round in both hands against his chest. Luke 3:14 is the only place in the four Gospels where soldiers come to the Forerunner and ask what they are to do, and the answer he gives is about their own trade and nobody else's: Do violence to no man, neither accuse any falsely; and be content with your wages. Between the two groups, at his feet, stands one small bare-trunked tree. Luke's hardest sentence in these verses is about such a tree — And now also the axe is laid unto the root of the trees — and no axe is painted at this one.",

"File:Baptism of Christ, Early XIV Century, St Mary Perivleptos Church, Ohrid Icon Gallery.jpg":
 "A panel of the early fourteenth century from St Mary Perivleptos at Ohrid, and the oldest of Luke's four Baptism icons, its gold ground split by a long crack but every figure on it whole. Christ stands waist-deep in dark water at the centre, and Luke puts him there almost incidentally, among a crowd that has already gone under: Now when all the people were baptized, it came to pass, that Jesus also being baptized, and praying, the heaven was opened. The clause makes him the last into the river, not the first, and the painter keeps him the same scale and the same bareness as any other man there — nothing marks him out but the halo. John leans in from the rocks at the left, his hand raised over Christ's head without touching it. Above, at the top edge, a scalloped grey arc is torn open in the gold, and a narrow beam falls from it toward Christ; the paint at its foot, where the dove itself belongs, has worn away to a small pale blur. Four haloed figures wait on the far bank at the right, heads bowed, their hands kept under their draped sleeves rather than bare. At the lower left, among bare wintry trees, a grey axe leans against a trunk — the Forerunner's own warning, given twelve verses earlier in this same chapter: and now also the axe is laid unto the root of the trees. Near it a small draped figure lies at the water's edge, turned back over its own shoulder as though in flight: the river given a body, an image from the Church's own hymn for this feast rather than from Luke's own sentence, which says only that the heaven was opened.",

"File:019 Baptism of Jesus Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg":
 "A Greek panel from the church of St Paraskevi at Langadas, worn hard enough that the two riverbanks have gone to flat green and the faces are mostly gone. What survives is the shape Luke's own sentence gives the scene: the heaven opened, painted as a torn grey-green cleft with small dark trees planted along both its slopes, and down the centre of it a broad orange-red shaft falls toward Christ's head, narrowing at its foot to a small worn mark too flattened by rubbing to call a bird with any confidence. John leans in at the left with his hand raised over him, not on him. At the right, two winged angels in gold and red stand one behind the other, their heads bowed and their own hands, covered by the fall of a white cloth held between them, not shown bare — the reverence the Gospel's own words do not describe, since Luke gives none of the actors here any gesture at all beyond a voice and a heaven that opens. Christ stands in dark water between the banks, and the single sentence Luke sets over the whole scene runs the length of two verses: Jesus also being baptized, and praying, the heaven was opened, and the Holy Ghost descended in a bodily shape like a dove upon him.",

"File:03 Baptism of Christ Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Adam.jpg":
 "A panel from the church of St Paraskevi at Adam, better preserved than its neighbour from Langadas and carrying, across the top in worn red letters, the remains of a title — legible now only as fragments of the Baptism … of Christ. Above Christ's head a white dove, wings spread and rayed with light, descends on a straight shaft out of a bank of grey cloud: the one icon of Luke's four where the bird itself, and not only the light around it, is still there to be seen — the Holy Ghost descended in a bodily shape like a dove upon him. John the Forerunner stands at the left, one hand laid on Christ's head, the other holding the end of his own cloak; three haloed angels wait at the right, the foremost holding a white cloth in both hands for the moment Christ will need it. Below the surface of the water, roots of the riverbank trees run down on both sides toward Christ's feet, tangled and bare, but no axe is cut into either trunk here — that detail belongs to the Ohrid panel, not this one. The water itself is dark and unpeopled beneath him; nothing in this telling personifies the river Luke never mentions by name at all in these two verses, only the heaven that opens above it.",

"File:0663Ha. Hermitage Museum (Hall 143). Icon of the Epiphany.jpg":
 "A Russian icon of the seventeenth century, in the Hermitage's collection, and the darkest of Luke's four: the whole centre of the panel is a single wedge of near-black water and sky fused into one shape, split at the top into two jagged banks of cloud with a white radiant star bursting between them. A thin silver ray runs straight down the middle of the black from that star to a second, smaller point of light just above Christ's head — the one clear passage, in any of these four icons, from the heaven that Luke says opened to the mark that stands for the dove that descended out of it, small and still uncertain in shape at this resolution, with two or three tiny red letters beside it too worn to read. John the Forerunner stands at the left in green, his hand on Christ's head; three winged angels lean in from the right in a diagonal file, the foremost in red holding a folded white cloth in both covered hands. Christ himself is thin, pale and marked with the dark lines of a body under strain, standing upright in the black water with his hands open at his sides — not lifted in prayer as some icons show him, though Luke's own clause insists that he was: Jesus also being baptized, and praying, the heaven was opened.",

"File:Christ's temptation (Monreale).jpg":
 "The same Monreale mosaic Matthew's and Mark's readers show, chosen here because it paints the one temptation all three Synoptics tell in the same order: the first. Luke's own opening sentence ties it directly to what has just happened at the Jordan in a way Matthew's does not — And Jesus being full of the Holy Ghost returned from Jordan, and was led by the Spirit into the wilderness — the same Spirit that had only just descended in a bodily shape now driving him out to fast and be tried before he does anything else. The mosaic gives the exchange in Latin around Christ and the winged, dark tempter reaching for the five stones between them: SI FILI DI ES DIC VT LAPIDES ISTI PANE FIANT — command these stones to become bread, if you are the Son of God. It is Matthew's own plural; Luke's devil asks for only one: If thou be the Son of God, command this stone that it be made bread. Luke's own wording of Christ's answer is shorter than Matthew's too: It is written, That man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word of God, stopping short of Matthew's addition that proceedeth out of the mouth of God. It is the first fall in a contest Luke alone tells in a different order from Matthew's — the mountain of the kingdoms comes second in this Gospel, the pinnacle of the temple third, where Matthew has them reversed. St Augustine, weighing the two orders against each other, judged it uncertain which Evangelist had kept the true sequence and which had arranged his own for effect; the Church has never had to choose between them.",

"File:Cathedral (Monreale) - Right wing transept - 2nd Temptation of Christ.jpg":
 "The panel that follows the first temptation along the same wall at Monreale, and in Luke's own order this is the last of the three, not the middle one Matthew makes it. The devil has set Christ on the roof of a small arcaded building — the pinnacle of the temple. Luke alone names the city beneath it: he brought him to Jerusalem. The Latin gives the last exchange of the contest: SI FILIUS DEI ES MITTE TE DEORSUM — if thou be the Son of God, cast thyself down from hence. Christ's reply stands on the other side: SCRIPTUM EST NON TEMPTABIS DOMINUM DEUM TUUM, the inscription's own it is written — though Luke's own third citation changes the verb the first two use: It is said, Thou shalt not tempt the Lord thy God. The winged tempter hangs beside Christ in the air, reaching toward him without touching, exactly as he offered the stones in the first scene. Between the two temptation scenes on this wall lies the one this reader has no icon for: taking him up into an high mountain, shewed unto him all the kingdoms of the world in a moment of time. What ends here is the whole contest, and Luke closes it with a warning the picture cannot show: when the devil had ended all the temptation, he departed from him for a season — not for good, and with none of Matthew's angels sent at once to minister to him. Luke gives him no such comfort here; the season would run until the Passion.",

"File:Monreale - Jesus heals Simon's mother in law.jpg":
 "The same Monreale mosaic Matthew's and Mark's readers show, and the Latin across the top already concedes Luke's own word before any Gospel text is quoted: IHS LIBERAT SOCRUM SIMONIS A MAGNIS FEBRIBUS, Jesus frees Simon's mother-in-law from a great fever. Magnis is Luke's own adjective; neither Matthew nor Mark qualifies the fever at all, and Simon's wife's mother was taken with a great fever is the physician's distinction Luke alone draws, a lesser fever set against a dangerous one. The gesture the mosaicist paints — Christ crossing the room with his arm already out toward her while she raises herself from the bed and reaches back, the two hands close but not joined across the gold — suits Luke's telling better than it suits the other two: Luke is the one Evangelist who has Christ neither touch her hand, as Matthew says, nor take her by the hand and lift her up, as Mark does. What Luke gives instead is a word alone: he stood over her, and rebuked the fever; and it left her — the same verb his Gospel uses elsewhere for silencing unclean spirits, and the fever obeys it as they do. The four men behind Christ are those who had just come with him out of the synagogue, and a fifth head looks in from the doorway at the upper right. Luke ends the verse where Matthew and Mark both end theirs, with what the healing was for: and immediately she arose and ministered unto them.",

// ---- chapter 5 ----

"File:Miraculous catch of fish - Sant'Apollinare Nuovo - Ravenna 2016.jpg":
 "A panel from the miracle cycle of Sant'Apollinare Nuovo at Ravenna, from the first years of the sixth century, catalogued on Commons as the Miraculous Draught of Fish and identified by a specialist in Christian iconography as the Calling of Peter and Andrew, the calling Matthew and Mark both open with one shared clause: casting a net into the sea: for they were fishers. Grey-haired and short-bearded, Peter leans out over the near gunwale with both hands already in the net; beside him, dark-haired and wilder, Andrew works the oar. A second man stands behind Christ on the bank, in a plain mantle, without a halo; who he is meant to be, neither the fresco nor this reading can say. St Augustine, in the Catena, sets the tellings side by side rather than choosing between them: Matthew and Mark here briefly state the matter, and how it was done. Luke explains it more at large. He also allows that the calling Matthew and Mark report may have come later, a second call after the one Luke tells here: so that afterwards that might happen which Matthew and Mark speak of. Luke's own telling gives a night's empty labour turned by Christ's word into a catch that fills two boats until they begin to sink, and Peter falling at Jesus' knees before the word that ends the story: Fear not; from henceforth thou shalt catch men. Christ stands on the bank here rather than seated with Peter in the boat, as Luke's own text has him through most of the story: he entered into one of the ships, which was Simon’s. The mosaic gives Andrew a face; Luke's own account of this calling never names him, a silence St Augustine notices in the same Catena block: he does not mention Andrew by name, who however is thought to have been in that ship, according to the accounts of Matthew and Mark.",

"File:Christ cleans leper man.jpg":
 "The same Monreale mosaic Matthew's and Mark's readers show, and the same Latin plea painted above the gold: POTES ME MVNDARE. Thou canst make me clean. Luke's own description of the man is the fullest of the three Synoptics: not a leper, as Matthew and Mark both have it, but a man full of leprosy, and the mosaicist has covered him with the disease from throat to ankle — dark spots scattered over his bare chest, his forearm, his shin — where Matthew's and Mark's own readings of this picture point only to the hand Christ reaches toward. He stands at the threshold of the building at the right, leaning on a staff, one hand lifted toward Christ and the other still open as though the plea were not yet finished; Christ comes from the left with his own hand out across the gold, the disciples pressed close behind him. Luke closes the story where Matthew and Mark do not: he withdrew himself into the wilderness, and prayed — the same habit of prayer this reader has already met at Christ's own baptism three chapters back, and one no icon of this scene paints.",

"File:Monreale - healing of paralytic.jpg":
 "The same Monreale mosaic Matthew's and Mark's readers show, and Luke's own word for what is happening along the top of the wall is neither of theirs: not Mark's roof dug open nor Matthew's men with a rope, but the tiling — they went upon the housetop, and let him down through the tiling with his couch into the midst before Jesus. Looked at closely, the two men lean in over a lattice of thin overlapping strips and work it apart with their bare hands; no rope is painted at this or any resolution, only fingers gripping the strips themselves. Christ sits enthroned at the left, his hand out; the paralytic has already come up off the bed onto his knees and reaches both arms back toward him, his feet still caught against the frame. Luke alone of the three gives Christ's opening word to him as a single term of address rather than a title of sonship: Man, thy sins are forgiven thee. Matthew and Mark both have Son instead. Luke alone also closes the miracle on the man's own voice: he departed to his own house, glorifying God; and they were all amazed, and they glorified God, and were filled with fear, saying, We have seen strange things to day. The scribes and Pharisees, in a tight knot of heads at the right, are Luke's own witnesses too: they had come out of every town of Galilee, and Judaea, and Jerusalem, drawn there before the miracle rather than only reacting to it.",

"File:Healing of the Paralytic 04-17.jpg":
 "The same Dionisy fresco Matthew's reader shows, from the Nativity cathedral at Ferapontov, and read here for the two clauses that belong to Luke alone. In the middle of the wall the man walks upright with his bed shouldered, and Luke's own sentence for the moment does not stop at the doorway the way Matthew's does: and immediately he rose up before them, and took up that whereon he lay, and departed to his own house, glorifying God. No other Evangelist gives him that last verb. At the right, where the fresco gathers a knot of witnesses with their heads drawn close together, is the moment Luke alone reports in full: they were all amazed, and they glorified God, and were filled with fear, saying, We have seen strange things to day. That is Luke's own word for it, a plain transliteration into English of the Greek paradoxa, things outside all expectation. Christ sits at the left with the apostles behind him, and below him the sick man still lies on his bed among the crowd that brought him — the getting up and the going out are Dionisy's whole subject on this wall, painted three times over so the eye cannot miss what Luke calls the strangest thing anyone there had seen.",

// ---- chapter 5, tier B: Levi ----

"File:090 Mathew the Apostle Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg":
 "A village panel of the nineteenth century from St Paraskevi at Langadas, the same portrait Matthew's and Mark's readers show: an old man with a long white beard, a codex on his knee and a reed pen in his hand. Luke names him only once, at the moment of the calling — he went forth, and saw a publican, named Levi, sitting at the receipt of custom — and never again in this Gospel by that name; the Church has always read Levi and Matthew as the same man, and this is his icon at the far end of the life that calling began, not a picture of the custom-house itself. Luke's own verse for what happened at the table is the one no other Evangelist writes in quite these words: he left all, rose up, and followed him — the same phrase, forsook all, Luke had already used of the fishermen four verses into this chapter, so that the tax-gatherer's rising and theirs are made to answer one another.",

"File:Matthew the Evangelist - icon.jpeg":
 "The same Palekh panel Matthew's and Mark's readers show, Levi painted as the Evangelist he became rather than as the publican Luke's own verse finds him: sitting at his desk with the quill lifted and the winged man — his symbol among the four living creatures — dictating at his shoulder. Luke's account of the call is the shortest clause of any Synoptic's, he left all, rose up, and followed him, and gives no word at all to what the icon spends its whole surface on, the writing of the book afterward. What Luke does give, and Matthew's own Gospel of this same day does not, is the feast that followed the rising: and Levi made him a great feast in his own house: and there was a great company of publicans and of others that sat down with them — his own house still open to the trade he had just left, on the same day he left it.",

// ---- chapter 6 ----

"File:Iomenos xeran echon cheira Dionysiou.jpg":
 "The same Dionysiou fresco Matthew's and Mark's readers show, read here for the one clause that belongs to Luke alone. Before Christ says a word to anyone, Luke tells what is happening that the fresco cannot paint: but he knew their thoughts, and said to the man which had the withered hand, Rise up, and stand forth in the midst — a sentence neither Matthew nor Mark gives at this point in the story, where both let the question about the sabbath come first and the knowledge of the watchers' minds go unmentioned. The man stands alone in the centre of the floor in his short red tunic, his arm already lifting; Christ sits at the left with the apostles behind him; three elders wait along the right wall, one with his hand raised in objection. Luke keeps Mark's next gesture — and looking round about upon them all — but not what Mark hangs on it, no anger and no grief at the hardness of their hearts, only the look itself and then the command: Stretch forth thy hand. And where Mark's men go out to the Herodians, Luke names no council and no ally at all: they were filled with madness; and communed one with another what they might do to Jesus — fury without a plan, in the verse where Mark already has one forming.",

"File:Synaxis of the Twelve Apostles by Constantinople master (early 14th c., Pushkin museum).jpg":
 "A panel painted in Constantinople in the early fourteenth century, now in Moscow, its title lettered in Greek across the top — Η ΣΥΝΑΞΙΣ ΤΩΝ ΔΩΔΕΚΑ ΑΠΟΣΤΟΛΩΝ, the Synaxis of the Twelve Apostles — the apostles' own names running on after it in the same band. They stand in two ranks, four in front with their haloes overlapping and eight behind, holding scrolls and Gospel books. It is a feast icon, kept on the thirtieth of June, set here over the choosing itself rather than any picture of what led to it. Mark's own account puts Christ on a mountain for this choosing too, but only Luke gives what he did there the whole night before it: he went out into a mountain to pray, and continued all night in prayer to God. And when it was day, he called unto him his disciples: and of them he chose twelve, whom also he named apostles. His own list keeps eleven of Matthew's and Mark's names and changes one: where they give Thaddaeus, Luke names Judas the brother of James, the same man tradition calls Jude. Luke also renders one apostle's surname differently from the other two: Simon called Zelotes, where Matthew and Mark both call him the Canaanite — the same word in its own Aramaic dress.",

"File:Synaxis of the Twelve Apostles Dimitar Molerov Rila Monastery 1835.jpg":
 "An icon of 1835 by Dimitar Molerov at the monastery of Rila in Bulgaria — the same close photograph Matthew's and Mark's readers show, five of the twelve filling the frame in a single rank, their gold haloes overlapping into an unbroken line, holding a white scroll, a Gospel bound in gold and stones, and, at the right, the small silver cross of a martyr. The Synaxis is the Church's feast for all twelve together, the thirtieth of June, and stands over the choosing rather than any picture of the mountain. What Luke's own account gives, and Matthew's and Mark's readings of this same icon do not, is the night before the choosing: he went out into a mountain to pray, and continued all night in prayer to God. Luke's own list changes one name from Matthew's: Judas the brother of James, not Thaddaeus.",

"File:The Twelve Apostles, with later gilding, Greek, Late Byzantine, early 1300s, tempera and gold leaf on wood panel - Princeton University Art Museum - DSC06701.jpg":
 "A late Byzantine panel of the early fourteenth century, tempera and gold leaf on wood, now in the Princeton University Art Museum, its gilding a later addition — the same photograph Matthew's and Mark's readers show, the twelve packed into three close ranks against gold until their haloes overlap, a company rather than twelve portraits. That crowding suits the verse just after Luke's own choosing better than the choosing itself: he came down with them, and stood in the plain, and the company of his disciples, and a great multitude of people out of all Judaea and Jerusalem, and from the sea coast of Tyre and Sidon, which came to hear him, and to be healed of their diseases. Luke's own list of the twelve, given three verses earlier, keeps eleven names Matthew and Mark also give and changes one: Judas the brother of James in place of their Thaddaeus.",

// ---- chapter 7 ----

"File:Iomenos Gon Ekatonarchou Dionysiou.jpg":
 "The same Dionysiou fresco Matthew's reader shows, from the katholikon on Mount Athos, 1547. Its own titulus names him son, where Matthew's own underlying word for him, παῖς, can mean either boy or servant — Matthew's own reading of this fresco already notes the painter has made that choice. Luke gives the same man one thing neither Matthew's Gospel nor this titulus does: a certain centurion’s servant, who was dear unto him, was sick, and ready to die. The fresco paints the centurion himself standing at a low wall directly opposite Christ, helmeted and in armour, one hand raised in speech — the meeting Matthew's own Gospel tells: there came unto him a centurion, beseeching him. Luke's account never once puts him in the room. He sent unto him the elders of the Jews, beseeching him that he would come and heal his servant. Only when Jesus was already near the house did he send friends a second time, and it is through them, not to Christ's own face, that Luke gives the centurion's best-known words: Lord, trouble not thyself: for I am not worthy that thou shouldest enter under my roof. Neither the elders nor the friends are painted; only the soldier himself, standing exactly where this Gospel never puts him. St Augustine, weighing Luke's account against Matthew's in the Catena, poses the difficulty as one question — how then will that be true which Matthew relates, A certain centurion came to him, seeing that he himself did not come? — and answers that Matthew made use of a general mode of expression: the more he believed the nearer he came. The elders' own plea is Luke's alone among the Synoptics, and gives the reason no other Gospel does: he loveth our nation, and he hath built us a synagogue. The servant himself sits up fair-haired on a red coverlet at the edge of the frame, face turned up, touched by no one in the picture — the one distance every Gospel that tells this story keeps, whoever carries the words there: say in a word, and my servant shall be healed.",

// ---- chapter 8 ----

"File:Representation of the Sower's parable.JPEG":
 "The same Romanian panel Mark's reader shows, its title lettered across the top, PILDA SEMĂNĂTORULUI — the parable of the sower — with the number 21 beside it, one panel in a series. The panel paints the sower with Christ's face under his own halo, walking forward with seed caught in the fold of his cloak, his arm swung out ahead of him and the throwing hand already open and empty. Luke's own words leave the figure unnamed — A sower went out to sow his seed — but the identification is not the painter's invention: the Catena's own comment on this opening clause already reads Christ's advent into it. St John Chrysostom writes of it, But Christ fitly denominates His advent, His going forth. At the left, on a bare trodden path, birds are down among the fallen seed, four on a close count, for Luke's own clause: some fell by the way side; and it was trodden down, and the fowls of the air devoured it — trodden down is Luke's own addition; the birds devouring the seed is in Matthew's and Mark's telling too. Bare grey rock fills the upper right; where Matthew and Mark both blame no depth of earth, Luke's own verse names a cause neither uses, moisture: it withered away, because it lacked moisture. A thicket with small red flowers grows along the right edge, for the thorns sprang up with it, and choked it; Luke's own explanation of that ground later names a third thing that chokes, missing from Matthew's two things and different from Mark's own third, the lusts of other things: pleasures of this life. Whether the dark, cone-shaped growth behind Christ's shoulder is meant for trees or stands for a ground of its own is not clear from the panel, and neither is a cluster of round pale shapes at the lower right, flecked like the fallen seed — stones, perhaps, or a harvest already reaped; this reading does not choose between them. What is plain is Luke's own name for the last ground: an honest and good heart, having heard the word, keep it, and bring forth fruit with patience — the heart, the keeping and the patience are his own words, though hearing the word and bringing forth fruit both echo Mark's own telling of this ground. Luke also holds back the count the other two give for it, keeping only the top of the yield: an hundredfold, with no thirty or sixty beside it.",

"File:Hrist utišava buru na moru, manastir Gračanica.jpg":
 "The same Gračanica fresco, about 1320, that Matthew's and Mark's readers show, from the monastery King Milutin built in Kosovo. Christ appears twice in the same boat, exactly as those readers already describe: standing at the left, one arm stretched out above the waves, and, apart at the right, resting his head on his hand as a man asleep. Luke's own account gives that sleep one plain verse and moves straight on to the storm: he fell asleep: and there came down a storm of wind on the lake; and they were filled with water, and were in jeopardy — the peril there, grammatically, is the men's own, where Mark's fuller telling gives it instead to the ship, now full. Between the two Christs a grey-bearded man stands at the mast, his face turned up toward the standing Christ; beside him another, in a red tunic, reaches up with both hands into the rigging. Luke's word for the water throughout these four verses is lake, never sea, painted here as ridge after ridge of curling grey brushwork under a sky gone almost black. Luke gives the disciples' own cry a shape neither Matthew nor Mark repeats, doubling the address itself: Master, master, we perish. Where Matthew and Mark both name the sea, Luke's own object is the wind and the raging of the water: and they ceased, and there was a calm. His question to them afterward is Luke's own wording too, Where is your faith?, and it is the disciples themselves who close the passage in wonder: they being afraid wondered, saying one to another, What manner of man is this! for he commandeth even the winds and water, and they obey him.",

"File:Mosaic of the exorcism of the Gerasene demoniac from the Basilica of Sant'Apollinare Nuovo.jpg":
 "The same Ravenna mosaic Mark's reader shows, from Sant'Apollinare Nuovo, about 520 — Christ painted young and beardless, a cross already in his halo, with one unhaloed companion at his shoulder. A single man kneels before him in a plain tan tunic, sleeved to the wrist, both arms reaching out: one man, not two, which is why the mosaic answers to Luke's own telling as it does to Mark's rather than to Matthew's pair. Luke's own account gives him, before Christ ever speaks, a description of his own: a certain man, which had devils long time, and ware no clothes, neither abode in any house, but in the tombs. The mosaic's man is clothed rather than naked; the dark hollow in the rock behind him could be read as the mouth of a tomb, or simply as shadow between the stones, and this reading does not decide it. The three swine at the right, already running down the bank into a strip of bright water, share the same single frame as the kneeling man, though in Luke's own order they run only after the asking and the sending: they besought him that he would not command them to go out into the deep, and, once let go, the herd ran violently down a steep place into the lake, and were choked. The mosaicist has painted one held moment for a scene Luke's own text spreads across several — the meeting, the naming, the plea, the drowning — and the man's calm, clothed kneeling reads more naturally as Luke's own ending, sitting at the feet of Jesus, clothed, and in his right mind, than as his condition when Christ arrived, even though the swine beside him have not yet finished their fall. Luke's own closing sentence gives the one healing two names: Christ's own charge names God, shew how great things God hath done unto thee, and the man's own preaching names Jesus, how great things Jesus had done unto him.",

"File:Haemorrhoissa Dionysiou.jpg":
 "A fresco of 1547 from the katholikon of Dionysiou on Mount Athos, painted by Tzortzis Phouka and confirmed by Commons' own description: a fresco of the healing of the Haemorrhoissa. Christ walks at the centre, haloed, ΙC ΧC lettered either side of his head, his own hand raised as he turns to speak: and Jesus said, Who touched me? A crowd of apostles presses close behind and beside him — the multitude throng thee and press thee, Peter's own answer to that question two verses later, though Peter is not himself distinguished by any inscription from the men around him. A second, smaller crowd is painted at the far left, apart from the first, in gold-brown robes: Luke gives no second crowd of his own, and this reading does not guess what the painter meant it to add. At the lower left a woman in a green mantle kneels and reaches a hand toward the hem of Christ's blue garment, in front of him rather than behind — Luke's own clause has her come behind him, and touch the border of his garment, a detail of approach the fresco simplifies so that her face and her reaching hand can both be shown at once. Above the scene a damaged inscription runs along the curve of the arch, too worn for this reading to transcribe past the subject Commons' own description already confirms.",

"File:Monreale - Healing of the Woman with an Issue of Blood.jpg":
 "A mosaic of the 1180s from Monreale Cathedral in Sicily. Christ stands at the centre in a blue mantle over gold, haloed, turning his head back over his shoulder toward the kneeling woman while his own apostles crowd in front of him, several with their hands raised in speech — Who touched me?, and their own answer, Master, the multitude throng thee and press thee. The woman kneels at the lower left in a green mantle, gazing up at Christ's face and reaching both hands toward the fold of his garment near his feet — again in front of him rather than behind, as in the Dionysiou fresco of the same scene, and for the same reason: a mosaic showing her face and her touch together cannot also show her at Christ's back. Immediately behind Christ, an elder in a brown mantle holds a white scroll in one hand; no inscription on this mosaic names him, so this reading does not call him Jairus, whose own request opens this same passage three verses earlier — nothing here marks this elder as that ruler of the synagogue rather than simply another of the crowd that throngs and presses. The gold ground behind every figure is the mosaicist's own convention, no part of Luke's own telling of either scene.",

"File:Daughter of the head of synagogue is resurrected by Christ.jpg":
 "The same Monreale mosaic Mark's reader shows, from Monreale Cathedral, the 1180s. The Latin inscription across the top runs IHS FILIAM IAYRI PRINCIPIS SYNAG..., cut off at the edge of this photograph, and continues onto a second line below it: Jesus raises the daughter of Jairus, ruler of the synagogue, in the house. The painter's own spelling, IAYRI, is not the classical Iairi; the name is Luke's own too, but from the passage before this one, which the picture does not illustrate. Christ approaches from the left with three unhaloed companions behind him, an elder grey-bearded, a dark-bearded man, and a fair youth — exactly the number, though not the names, of he suffered no man to go in, save Peter, and James, and John, and the father and the mother of the maiden. The room the mosaicist has built for them holds far more than that admitted five: a crowd of mourning women and bearded men, one gripping a book, presses in behind the bed on every side, which sits uneasily beside Luke's own he put them all out. The girl herself sits upright already, dressed in a jewelled gown, her hand clasped in Christ's at the bed's edge: took her by the hand, and called, saying, Maid, arise. A bearded man stands at the bed's head with one hand pressed to his breast, closer to a father's own grief than to a ruler's petition, and behind him a woman's hand is at her own cheek — a grief the mosaic has not yet let go of, though Luke's own next clause has already moved past it: her spirit came again, and she arose straightway.",

"File:Raising of Jairus' daughter 03-19.jpg":
 "The same Ferapontov fresco Matthew's and Mark's readers show, Dionisy's work of 1502 on a curved wall, painted twice over with the one scene as those two readers already describe: Christ arriving at the right, and Christ at the bedside at the left. At the right, hand raised in speech, with a cluster of golden-haloed apostles behind him; small at his feet, one or two more kneeling figures — Matthew's reader counts two — belong to the moment Luke sets in the passage before this one: he fell down at Jesus’ feet, and besought him that he would come into his house, a verse belonging to the passage before this one, 8:40–48, which this reader illustrates with its own two icons of the woman with the issue of blood rather than with this fresco. At the left the girl sits up already in a white hood and gown, her own hand raised to meet Christ's, the two overlapping at the wrist: took her by the hand, and called, saying, Maid, arise. Mark's reader says the face inside the haloed figure at the bed has flaked away entirely, leaving a bare ring of ochre; at this resolution it has not vanished so much as dimmed to a faint brown ghost, its features still just traceable, nearer Matthew's own barely legible than to a bare ring. A veiled woman and a bearded man stand together at the bed's head, looking down at the girl — Matthew's reader calls them the parents, and Luke names both as the two Christ did admit: and the father and the mother of the maiden.",

"File:Tokalı Kilise Raising of daughter of Jairus - 2004 6964.jpg":
 "The same fresco Matthew's and Mark's readers show, painted on the rock face of a cave church at Göreme in the tenth century — the oldest of the three Jairus images, and on this wall the most worn. Its coverlet dominates the lower half of the composition, patterned in dark red scrollwork edged with a band of black zigzags, drawn with more care than anything else on the wall. The child herself cannot be seen to have a hand at all: she lies swathed to the crown in a striped wrapping of blue and white, and both Matthew's reading and this one agree on what that means for the scene — there is nothing of hers left for Christ's own hand to close over. He leans toward her from the left, a cross still traceable in his halo, one companion's plain halo crowding close behind his own. Further left again, past the edge of a painted doorway, an unhaloed man raises one hand; a run of Greek letters beside him has worn past this reading's ability to make out a name, so none is given here. Beyond the crowd gathered at the bed, a second cross-marked halo appears — Christ, on this reading, a second time on this same stretch of wall, though whether this is the same moment or the next scene along it, none of the three readings of this fresco settles. Luke's own sentence for what happens at the bed carries no word in a second language, unlike Mark's Aramaic: he put them all out, and took her by the hand, and called, saying, Maid, arise.",

// ---- chapter 9 ----

"File:Evlogesis Pente Arton Dionysiou.jpg":
 "The same Dionysiou fresco of 1547 that Matthew's, Mark's and John's readers show, inscribed along the top Η ΕΥΛΟΓΗΣΙΣ ΤΩΝ ΠΕΝΤΕ ΑΡΤΩΝ — the blessing of the five loaves. Luke alone names whose ground this desert place is: he took them, and went aside privately into a desert place belonging to the city called Bethsaida, a detail neither Matthew nor Mark gives here. Christ stands at the left, ΙΣ ΧΣ lettered by his halo, his eyes already lifted past the man before him into the night above them both — looking up to heaven, he blessed them, and brake — his own bare hand cupping the five stacked loaves, the two fishes resting on top of the pile; the grey-bearded apostle before him leans in toward the pile, though his hands are lost in the fold of his own sleeve, and whether he has yet closed on it the picture does not quite show. Behind Christ a jagged white mountain climbs into the dark; one Gospel does put a mountain at this same miracle, Jesus went up into a mountain, and there he sat with his disciples, but it is John's, and Luke never mentions one. Across the middle ground an apostle in green carries bread out to a crowd seated in ordered rows on the slope; among them sits a fair-haired, bare-headed figure, woman or youth, and a boy stands apart with one hand raised — neither counted in Luke's own bare tally of the day, for they were about five thousand men. The ranks themselves keep Luke's own instruction: he said to his disciples, Make them sit down by fifties in a company — his own number is fifties alone, not Mark's hundreds and fifties, and the painter has set the crowd out in blocks to answer it. At the lower left a stack of round loaves in wicker baskets comes to five, by a close count, short of Luke's own twelve baskets gathered afterward of fragments that remained — Mark's reader counts four in the same stack, Matthew's about half a dozen; the painting has not made the number easy to settle twice the same way.",

"File:Feeding the multitude, Sant'Apollinare Nuovo, Ravenna.jpg":
 "The same mosaic Matthew's and Mark's readers show, sixth-century work from Sant'Apollinare Nuovo at Ravenna, kept down to five figures on gold. Both arms are already held out from the body at the centre, one to either side; the face between them is young and beardless, a small cross fixed inside the halo behind it. On the left, a pair of hands lift a stack of bread, a cross scored into each round loaf; on the right, two dark fish are held out together, gripped rather than caught mid-exchange. A narrow strip of green runs along the foot of the whole scene, a plant set at either end of it — the one piece of ground the mosaicist allows the miracle, and no crowd anywhere on it. Luke's own verb for the moment carries a word neither Matthew nor Mark gives it: he blessed them, and brake, and gave to the disciples to set before the multitude — both of theirs leave the object of the blessing unsaid, bare blessed, and brake, and only Luke says what was blessed. That plain pronoun sits close to what the gold panel itself shows: hands already closing over the bread and the fish, with no multitude anywhere in the frame still waiting to be fed.",

"File:Transfiguration of Christ Icon Sinai 12th century.jpg":
 "Luke dates this ascent differently from Matthew and Mark, about an eight days after these sayings rather than their six, and gives it a purpose neither states, to pray, with the company in an order that puts John ahead of James: he took Peter and John and James, and went up into a mountain to pray. This detail, cut from a wide Sinai festal beam whose gold ground is worn through in patches across the whole board, keeps the composition every other icon here shares: Christ on the peak between the two, one hand raised, a scroll in the other. Elias, at the left, is wrapped in a dark mantle, one hand showing half-open at his waist; Moses, at the right, is younger, holds a pale tablet against himself, and reaches his free hand toward Christ. Below, where the wear is worst, three men are scattered across the damaged gold: one seated with a hand lifted as if answering something, one bowed flat at the centre, one kneeling with a hand near his own chest. The seated figure's raised hand is as near as the panel comes to Peter's offer, and Luke's own qualifier for it comes in the same breath, not after it: Master, it is good for us to be here: and let us make three tabernacles; one for thee, and one for Moses, and one for Elias: not knowing what he said.",

"File:Icon of the Transfiguration from Saint Catherine's, Sinai.jpg":
 "Behind Christ's head on this second Sinai panel there is no disc of gold at all, only a few pale red lines fading out into the same bare ground the rest of the board is painted on — of the four Transfigurations in this gallery, the one that risks the least on light drawn in around him. What carries the glory instead is the clothing, and the Evangelist whose own word for it this icon paints most exactly is Luke: his raiment was white and glistering, glistering being his alone, where Matthew has white as the light and Mark exceeding white as snow, so as no fuller on earth can white them. At the left a grey head is wrapped away almost entirely in a dark mantle; at the right stands a much younger figure, dark hair loose, one hand out toward Christ, no tablet in it this time, unlike the same two painted on the panel beside this one — his other hand is lost in the fall of his own robe. Three men are strung along a narrow seam of light below: nearest the mountain's edge, still on his knees, one hand caught mid-gesture, is Peter; further down, gone entirely into the folds of his own robe, is John, no face left to see; apart from the other two, seated rather than kneeling, one hand loose on a raised knee, watching sideways, is James. This gallery's habit of putting all three men down at the sight — kneeling, folded over, seated, none of them standing — reads nearer to Matthew's own report, they fell on their face, than to anything Luke says happened to them there — Luke gives no fall at all. What he gives instead comes earlier, before the glory is even seen: Peter and they that were with him were heavy with sleep: and when they were awake, they saw his glory, and the two men that stood with him. Sleep first, then waking, then glory — and this panel's own middle figure, given no gesture and no face, is as near as paint comes to showing which of the three that sleep sat heaviest on.",

"File:Icon of transfiguration (Spaso-Preobrazhensky Monastery, Yaroslavl).jpg":
 "The most crowded of the four Transfigurations in this gallery, and the only one that paints anything Luke reports beyond the mountaintop itself. The light around Christ at the centre is spiked into eight points, dark blue-green with long gold rays breaking through them; at the left Elias leans out from the rock, hand lifted mid-gesture; at the right Moses leans from another ledge, both arms wrapped around an object bound in cloth rather than opened. Both Matthew and Mark also report the two men talking with Christ; only Luke gives the subject of it, and this panel's own choice to keep Moses's object closed sits well beside what he alone reports next: who appeared in glory, and spake of his decease which he should accomplish at Jerusalem. At the foot of the mountain, one apostle kneels with both arms thrown up toward the light, his face still lifted to it; of the other two, one is pitched head-first down the slope, a hand at his own face, and the other is down on hands and knees, face turned away and hidden. Two small scenes flank the mountain that none of the other three icons here paint at all: at one side, Christ leading the three up the slope on the way there; at the other, Christ bringing them down again — the next passage, and Luke alone gives it its own day: on the next day, when they were come down from the hill, much people met him.",

"File:042 Transfiguration of Jesus Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg":
 "Signed along the bottom edge — 1885 · χείρ Σταύρου Μαργαρίτη εκ Κολακιάς — this one panel reverses the composition every other Transfiguration in this gallery repeats. It is Moses who kneels on the left this time, an open pale tablet on his knee and his own eyes on it rather than on Christ; the old man with the full white beard, one hand curled at his own breast, kneels opposite him on the right — Elias. They are the same two Luke names taking part in the vision, there talked with him two men, which were Moses and Elias; what the two spoke of is given on the Yaroslavl icon in this same gallery and is not repeated here. Light gathers around Christ near the top of the panel, egg-shaped and pale rather than a full disc, his right hand lifted to bless and a scroll closed in the other. Three men below have each been thrown a different way: one has flung both arms straight up while still on his knees; a second lies full length, face to the sky, in the middle of the slope; a third has recoiled hardest of all, at the far edge, and beside his own halo the letters ΙΑΚ still hold — James, named by the painter where the other two are not.",

// ---- chapter 11 ----

"File:Christos Iomenos Daimonon Takophon Dionysiou.jpg":
 "The same Dionysiou fresco of 1547 that Matthew's reader shows at 9:32-34, its own dumb demoniac — Ο Χ(ΡΙΣΤΟ)Σ ΙΩΜΕΝΟΣ ΤΟΝ ΔΑΙΜΟΝΩΝ ΤΑΚΩΦΟΝ lettered across the top in three lines, the word for his affliction broken by the line itself. That earlier passage of Matthew's is not what the Catena itself sets beside this healing. Bede reads it as the same miracle Matthew tells at 12:22, the only demoniac of Matthew's whom he calls both blind and dumb together: that demoniac is related by Matthew to have been not only dumb, but blind. Three miracles then were performed at the same time on one man — and the same dispute about Beelzebub follows there too. This fresco, borrowed from Matthew's shorter, earlier notice of a dumb demoniac alone, keeps a dumb man and no blindness, which is all Luke himself says of him: a devil, and it was dumb. Theophylact's own comment on the word explains the ear the painter has chosen for Christ's hand to rest on, not the head or the mouth: He who was brought before the Lord was both dumb in speech, and deaf in hearing. He was casting out a devil, and it was dumb. And it came to pass, when the devil was gone out, the dumb spake; and the people wondered — and this fresco gives that wonder to a second man, standing apart in an archway of his own, both hands lifted, palms open, no halo on him. A crowd of apostles presses close at Christ's shoulder; the healed man himself is caught bending forward, a green tunic under a red mantle, a hand pressed to his chest, set against a plain wall — nothing about him showing what he was a moment before, since Luke's own account describes him not at all, only the one word, dumb. What follows in Luke's own text, some of them said, He casteth out devils through Beelzebub the chief of the devils, and everything Christ answers it with — a kingdom divided against itself, a house divided against a house, the finger of God, the strong man armed — belongs to the rest of the passage, and none of it is painted here.",

// ---- chapter 15 ----

"File:The Good Shepherd from the Imperial Palace of Constantinople 5th-6th century AD.jpg":
 "No halo, no cross, nothing to mark this figure for Christ at all — a country vignette worked into a palace floor's plain white tesserae, fifth or sixth century, the same one Matthew's reader shows. A beast is draped across his own shoulders, its legs gripped fore and aft by his two hands, and a woven basket of two pale rounded shapes rests at his side. The pose itself is centuries older than the parable it has come to stand for, the ancient ram-bearer, a shepherd with a ram across his shoulders, and the Church took the whole shape over rather than invent one of its own. What that borrowed pose happens to show, though, answers to Luke's own wording of this parable more exactly than to Matthew's: only Luke gives the shepherd this clause, he layeth it on his shoulders, rejoicing, where Matthew's own telling of the same parable never mentions a shoulder at all. The two Gospels also place the flock and the search differently: Luke leaves the ninety and nine standing in the wilderness while the shepherd goes after the one that is lost; Matthew instead sends the shepherd himself into the mountains to seek it. Wilderness or mountains, any rejoicing — none of it is in this worn floor; it stands for the word shepherd rather than picturing the parable. What drew the parable out of Christ, in Luke's own Gospel only, was a complaint about the company he kept: then drew near unto him all the publicans and sinners for to hear him. And the Pharisees and scribes murmured, saying, This man receiveth sinners, and eateth with them.",

"File:Ravenna — The Good Shepherd mosaic.jpg":
 "A gold-framed lunette from the tomb built for Galla Placidia at Ravenna, about 425, the same one Matthew's reader shows, set under a night-blue vault scattered with gold rosettes like stars. The one seated inside the frame is young and beardless, dressed in gold and violet with a full disc of light behind his head; what stands upright in his left hand is not a shepherd's crook but a cross, and his right hand touches the nose of the nearest animal. Five more stand or lie on the green ground around his rock, six in all, three either side, and not one has its head down to graze — every face is turned to his. No shoulder carries anything here; that gesture belongs to the plainer floor in this same gallery. Neither does this gold court picture the parable's own ending any more than the floor pictures its opening — no home, no gathered friends and neighbours, no one saying Rejoice with me; for I have found my sheep which was lost — but the six watching faces sit well enough over the joy Luke names last of all: I say unto you, that likewise joy shall be in heaven over one sinner that repenteth, more than over ninety and nine just persons, which need no repentance.",

// ---- chapter 19 ----

"File:Baiophoros Dionysiou.jpg":
 "White letters against the dark sky above this 1547 Dionysiou fresco give its own title, Η ΒΑΙΟΦΟΡΟΣ — the name the Church gives the feast, not a word out of any Gospel's own text. One animal only carries Christ here, who blesses with his right hand while the company behind him presses close under a run of gold haloes; above them, a full green palm holds two boys at once, working branches loose and passing them down, and across the scene Jerusalem's own towers wait with its people already spilling out through the gate. Not one clause of that tree is Luke's: his own account of this ride never names a branch, a palm, or a leaf, only cloth in the road, and as he went, they spread their clothes in the way — they, in Luke's own sentence, being the disciples who had just set him on the colt, not a crowd. That branch-cutting is a detail this fresco shares with the Langadas and Athonite panels in the gallery; the Adam panel's own palm has nobody in it. All three borrow the scene from Mark's and Matthew's telling, or from John's own naming of the palm outright. What belongs to Luke alone in this stretch of road is its direction: he went before, ascending up to Jerusalem — Christ, not the company behind him, setting the pace.",

"File:005 Entry into Jerusalem Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg":
 "Red letters at the upper left carry the same feast-name lettered on the fresco beside this one in the gallery, Η ΒΑΙΟΦΟΡΟΣ. Here the white mount is turned so that Christ's own head faces backward toward the company following him rather than ahead toward the walls waiting for him — the reverse of the phrase Luke's own text uses for this stretch of road, he went before, ascending up to Jerusalem, Christ leading out ahead of the very men this panel has him watching instead. The errand behind the scene is already finished in Luke's own telling: two men were sent for this same colt, and they that were sent went their way, and found even as he had said unto them — it is the procession itself this panel paints, not the sending or the finding. One dark palm stands in the middle of the board, a small figure worked into its branches, the detail the reading beside this one in the gallery already marks as absent from Luke's own words entirely. Cloth already lies flat on the ground under the advancing hooves, which is the one clause of this whole scene Luke's own text does give: they spread their clothes in the way.",

"File:18 Triumphal entry into Jerusalem Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Adam..jpg":
 "A low mountain range sits behind the walls in this 1846 panel from the village of Adam, and a single small palm stands at the left — but nobody is in it. That empty palm sets this panel apart from the three other Entry icons in this gallery, each of which puts someone in its tree. Jerusalem itself takes up the whole top half of the board, towers and a gold dome already close enough to touch, and it is toward that city, not back at the three haloed men following him, that Christ's own face is turned — unlike the Langadas panel beside it in the gallery, where the same gesture runs the other way. Luke alone marks exactly where this moment falls on the road: when he was come nigh, even now at the descent of the mount of Olives — the walls already this large because so little of the road is left to cover. A child is down on his knees at the animal's own hooves, a dark cloth already spread there and another garment laid beside it; at the far right a lone figure in pink leans out high above the crowd at the gate, one arm stretched out to the left.",

"File:Entry into Jerusalem (Afon icon).jpg":
 "Sixteenth-century work from Athos, and the fullest of the four Entry panels in this gallery: a packed file of haloed men at the left, a boy working a branch loose from the dark tree at the centre — again, no such branch is anywhere in Luke's own account of this ride — and the entire right side of the board given over to Jerusalem, row on row of onlooking faces crowded at its gate. That gate crowd paints a city coming out to meet him, which is nearer to John's own account of this day than to Luke's; Luke's own acclamation comes from neither city nor tree but from the packed file at the left itself: not any multitude at large, but the whole multitude of the disciples, who took up praising God with a loud voice for all the mighty works that they had seen, calling out Blessed be the King that cometh in the name of the Lord: peace in heaven, and glory in the highest. That cometh in the name of the Lord is a line all four Gospels share; what the crowd itself cries in Luke alone, among the three Synoptics, is the King in place of Matthew's son of David and Mark's kingdom of our father David, no Hosanna at all, and the pairing peace in heaven, and glory in the highest, which answers back across his own Gospel to its opening pages, where angels sang the same two words in the other order to shepherds in a field: glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace. None of what comes right after this in Luke's own text — Pharisees demanding Christ silence his disciples, and his answer, that if these should hold their peace, the stones would immediately cry out — is painted anywhere in this gallery.",

"File:Christ banish tradesmen from Temple (Monreale).jpg":
 "The same Monreale mosaic Matthew's and Mark's readers show. Next to an older, grey-haired figure at the left stands another man, unhaloed, one arm raised with a short rod closed in his fist; off to the right a wooden dove-cage hangs from the end of a long pole that runs down to one of the sellers, and hoofed animals crowd the lower right corner. None of that belongs to Luke's own account of the act, which stops two clauses short of any of it: he went into the temple, and began to cast out them that sold therein, and them that bought. Mark opens his own telling with the identical verb — began to cast out them that sold and bought — so the difference is not in how forcefully Luke's Christ acts, only in how soon his sentence ends: no table overturned, no money-changer named, no dove-seller, no ox. What follows in Luke, and in nothing this scene paints, is a habit rather than an act — he taught daily in the temple — met at once by a plot no single gesture could freeze: the chief priests and the scribes and the chief of the people sought to destroy him. Luke does not stand alone in explaining why it failed; Mark gives the same shape a few verses after his own telling of this same cleansing, the crowd's own astonishment stopping the same men. But Luke's own wording of it is his: and could not find what they might do: for all the people were very attentive to hear him. Cyril of Alexandria, on this clause, sets the crowd's devotion squarely against the leaders' unbelief: the people held Christ in far higher estimation than the Scribes and Pharisees, and chiefs of the Jews, who not receiving the faith of Christ themselves, rebuked others.",

"File:Rossano Gospels - Cleansing of the Temple.jpg":
 "The same leaf from the Rossano Gospels that Matthew's and Mark's readers already show — silver script on purple parchment, one of the earliest illustrated Gospel books to survive — read here as a page of witness rather than one plain scene. The upper band paints the act itself, headed ΠΕΡΙ ΤΩΝ ΕΚΒΛΗΘΕΝΤΩΝ ΕΚ ΤΟΥ ΙΕΡΟΥ, concerning those cast out of the temple, with a money-changer, a dove-cage and driven oxen all given a place in the picture though none of them has one in Luke's own two verses: them that sold therein, and them that bought is the whole of his description. Below, four busts stand witness in a row, each with his own column of text. The crowned bust at the left is David, and the same crown and the same overlined caption recur at the third bust — David quoted twice, with Hosea between his two appearances and Isaiah after them. Isaiah is the one whose prophecy the scene above is built on, Isaiah 56:7 behind My house is the house of prayer — but where Matthew and Mark both keep the prophecy's own future tense, shall be called, Luke alone flattens it to a plain is. Jeremiah, the source of the sentence's second half, den of thieves, has no column among the manuscript's four witnesses. Bede, writing on this same block of the Catena, offers three reasons the leaders wanted him dead without choosing between them — his daily teaching, the traders he had just cast out, or the praise that had already met him at the gate — and Luke's own next clause only says that they sought to destroy him and could not find what they might do, for all the people were very attentive to hear him, a shape Mark gives too, in his own words, a few verses after his own account of this same act.",

// ---- chapter 21 ----

"File:Second Coming by G.Klontzas (16th c.).jpg":
 "Georgios Klontzas painted this Cretan icon of the Deutera Parousia in the late sixteenth century; Matthew's and Mark's readers already show the same board for their own versions of Christ's own Olivet sermon. Christ sits in a mandorla near the top, and that detail alone gives this reading solid ground: Luke's own noun for the moment is singular where the other two Synoptics are not — and then shall they see the Son of man coming in a cloud with power and great glory — one cloud, against Matthew's clouds of heaven and Mark's own plural. Everything below the throne — ranked saints, a wide flame, ground opening on its dead at the bottom edge — belongs to the Church's own picture of the Last Judgment more than to any one Evangelist's nine verses; nothing here is drawn specifically from what Luke goes on to say. Two things that are Luke's own among the Synoptics have no clear counterpart here. His own list of signs adds a sea roaring that neither Matthew's nor Mark's parallel names — the sea and the waves roaring — a detail the dead-rising register at the foot of the panel, bare ground rather than any sea, does not touch either way. And where Matthew and Mark both keep the parable's tree singular, Ambrose notices that Luke widens it: Matthew speaks of the fig-tree only, Luke of all the trees — a distinction this Last Judgment does not set out to illustrate at all. What the icon spends most of its space on, no part of it attempts the way Luke's own text in these verses actually closes: heaven and earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away.",

// ---- chapter 22 ----

"File:Deipnos Mystikos Dionysiou.jpg":
 "Mark's reader already shows this 1547 Dionysiou fresco, Ο ΔΕΙΠΝΟΣ Ο ΜΥΣΤΙΚΟΣ lettered above the table. Among the jugs, knives and more than one goblet already set on the cloth, a footed bowl at the centre holds a single fish — the painter's own addition — Luke's own words for how the meal began go no further than they made ready the passover, and give no dish a name. What the crowded cloth does not sort out is the shape Luke's own telling gives this table across his seven verses: two cups, not one. The first comes before the bread is even touched — he took the cup, and gave thanks, and said, Take this, and divide it among yourselves — sealed at once with a vow, I will not drink of the fruit of the vine, until the kingdom of God shall come. Only then does Luke report the bread — and he took bread, and gave thanks, and brake it, and gave unto them, saying, This is my body which is given for you — followed by a second cup, likewise also the cup after supper, saying, This cup is the new testament in my blood. Gave thanks is Luke's own verb for both bread and cup alike; Matthew and Mark bless the bread and keep gave thanks for the cup only. And where a man in this fresco leans in with his arm toward the bowl, Luke's own sign for that same moment names no dish at all — the hand of him that betrayeth me is with me on the table — the dipping into a dish belonging to Matthew's and Mark's own telling of the warning, not to his.",

"File:05 Last Supper Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Adam.jpg":
 "Asterios Anagnostos signed and dated this panel 1898, from the church of Ayia Paraskevi in the village of Adam — the same one Matthew's and Mark's readers already show — inscribed ὁ δεῖπνος ὁ μυστικός, the Mystical Supper. Christ's hand is lifted, palm out; at his side the disciple John's own Gospel calls beloved leans in, his head low — a figure and a posture that belong to John's telling, not Luke's, whose own account of this meal names no one at the table until the warning to Simon a few verses further on. Two small cups sit apart on the cloth, one to either flank of the great bowl at the centre, a dark flask standing beside the cup at the left — a pair that happens to match an arithmetic that is Luke's alone among the Synoptics: two cups, not one, the first before the bread is broken, he took the cup, and gave thanks, and said, Take this, and divide it among yourselves, the second after it, likewise also the cup after supper, saying, This cup is the new testament in my blood. A sleeve reaches toward the great bowl at the centre from the right, though Luke's own words for this same warning name no bowl and no dish at all: behold, the hand of him that betrayeth me is with me on the table. The dish belongs to Matthew's and Mark's own telling of this warning, not to Luke's sentence for it.",

"File:Kirillo-Belozersky iconostasis 12 - Last Supper.jpg":
 "The same 1497 panel from the festival tier at Kirillo-Belozersky that Matthew's and Mark's readers already show. Christ sits apart, at the table's own upper corner rather than its centre, the board tipped forward so nothing on the cloth is hidden from a viewer looking up at it. Bread lies in small triangular pieces across the pale cloth; one is already held between an apostle's fingers at the far side, the closest this icon comes to painting Luke's own he took bread, and gave thanks, and brake it, and gave unto them. Three vessels sit along the table's edge: a footed cup toward one end, a lidded jar between it and the far end, and there, a cup whose outline was only ever drawn, never filled in with paint. Whatever is missing there, whether by the plan of 1497 or five centuries of wear since, the two cups that remain — one finished, one only sketched — sit closer to an arithmetic that is Luke's alone among the four Gospels than a single cup would: two cups over this meal, the first before the bread, he took the cup, and gave thanks, and said, Take this, and divide it among yourselves, the second after it, likewise also the cup after supper, saying, This cup is the new testament in my blood. One apostle leans across and lowers his hand toward the footed cup, near where Luke's own warning sits, though Luke's own sentence for it puts the hand on the table, not in any vessel: the hand of him that betrayeth me is with me on the table. Two apostles at the front face one another with empty, open hands, a gesture this icon cannot date within Luke's own seven verses for the meal, since his account still has Simon warned by name and two swords produced before the company ever leaves this table.",

"File:Gethsemane Dionysiou.jpg":
 "A 1547 fresco from the katholikon of Dionysiou on Mount Athos, running along a rocky ridge above a great arched niche cut into the wall, titled across the night sky in white letters, Η ΠΡΟΣΕΥΧΗ ΤΟΥ ΧΥ — the Prayer of Christ. The same haloed figure climbs the ridge four times: palms flat on the stone in the first pose, head tipped back with a hand pressed against his face in the second, body folded down with an arm reaching toward the rock in the third, and on his feet again in the fourth, turned to face the sleepers with one arm out over them. What the fresco spreads across three kneeling figures, Luke tells as a single unbroken prayer: he was withdrawn from them about a stone’s cast, and kneeled down, and prayed — and what follows is not a second departure but the same prayer growing more urgent, being in an agony he prayed more earnestly — so where Matthew's own three goings and three returns give the painter's convention to follow (Mark narrates only two of the three prayers), Luke's prayer only deepens, it does not repeat. Hard to pick out against the black ground, a small haloed shape with wings hovers near the fresco's own title, above the second, upright figure: there appeared an angel unto him from heaven, strengthening him — Luke's verse alone among the four Gospels. Past the black arch below the ridge, a crowd of sleeping men lies heaped together, but one of them sits upright among the rest, in orange, both hands raised open toward the Lord. Luke's own reason for their sleep is not the one Matthew and Mark both give, their eyes were heavy — he found them sleeping for sorrow — and it is the whole company he speaks to here, not one man turned to and named, as Peter is in Matthew's telling and Simon in Mark's: Why sleep ye? rise and pray, lest ye enter into temptation.",

"File:Cathedral (Monreale) - Right wing transept - Agony in the Garden.jpg":
 "Byzantine craftsmen set this mosaic into the cathedral's right transept at Monreale, Sicily, in the twelfth century, against a ground of gold. Near the top Christ kneels partway up a bare, gold-washed rise, his hands lifted together, and a great angel enters from the edge of the gold, wings spread, one hand reaching down to him — the plainest painting of Luke's own angel that this reader shows anywhere: there appeared an angel unto him from heaven, strengthening him, a verse Luke gives and no other Evangelist. Below the kneeling Christ the hillside is packed with sleeping men, slumped over one another under their mantles. A Latin titulus is cut into the gold near the top edge, badly damaged; what can still be made out of it answers to the warning Matthew and Mark both give at this point — watch and pray, in Matthew's own words — where Luke drops watch and keeps pray, giving it twice over instead: Pray that ye enter not into temptation before the prayer, and rise and pray, lest ye enter into temptation after it. Further along the same scene Christ appears once more, one hand keeping a rolled scroll at his side, the other stretched out toward the sleeping men — one of whom, an old man with a pale beard cut close, sits upright and awake, one hand raised to his own chest, his face turned to meet the returning Christ, while the rest sleep on. Luke's own sentence for this return singles out no one apostle — he found them sleeping for sorrow — and what he says to them is spoken to the whole company at once, not to one man turned to and named, as Peter is in Matthew's telling of the same return and Simon in Mark's: Why sleep ye? rise and pray, lest ye enter into temptation.",

"File:The Lord's prayer in Gethsemane - Stavronikita monastery, Mt Athos - Theophanes of Crete, 16th c..jpg":
 "Theophanes the Cretan painted this Prayer in Gethsemane, sixteenth-century work for the katholikon at Stavronikita on the Holy Mountain; the plaster along the top edge has flaked back to bare stone, most of whatever title once ran there now lost. Christ climbs the rock four times: in the first pose his head is bent so far down that only the rim of the halo shows, his face lost against the stone; in the second he stands straight, halo turned full toward the viewer, at the crown of the ridge; in the third he kneels at the far side with his head thrown back; and in the fourth, lower on the rock, he is on his feet again with one arm reaching down toward the sleepers. Luke's own text asks for none of this threefold repetition: he was withdrawn from them about a stone’s cast, and kneeled down, and prayed — a single prayer, and what grows more intense is that same prayer, being in an agony he prayed more earnestly, not three risings and returns, the pattern this fresco borrows from Matthew's own count (Mark narrates only two of the prayers). Unlike the two other paintings of the same scene in this reader, no angel can be made out anywhere in what survives of the sky above the four figures — the plaster there is badly damaged, and nothing legible in it answers to there appeared an angel unto him from heaven, strengthening him, a detail Luke alone among the four Gospels gives. Below, the sleepers lie every way a body can give way to sleep — one hidden entirely inside a red cloak but for his face, another lying full length in a plain green robe, a third sitting upright in yellow with a hand still raised. Luke's own reason for their sleep is not the one Matthew and Mark both give, their eyes were heavy — he found them sleeping for sorrow — and what Christ says to them is spoken to the whole company, no one apostle named: Why sleep ye? rise and pray, lest ye enter into temptation.",

// ---- 22:47-53, the arrest ----

"File:Prodosia Dionysiou.jpg":
 "Dionysiou's katholikon on Mount Athos carries this fresco from 1547 — the same wall Matthew's and Mark's readers already show — Η ΠΡΟΔΟΣΙΑ lettered white across the dark sky above it, the Betrayal. Luke will not simply name the deed; he first names the man, and names him coldly: he that was called Judas, one of the twelve — a formula Cyril of Alexandria reads as holding the very name in something like abhorrence, one of the twelve making the enormity of it worse rather than smaller. Both his arms close round the Lord out of a fold of red drapery over green, and his own face is drawn level with Christ's; the Lord, haloed, stands motionless in the one still point of a wall crowded with movement, and does not pull away. What Luke's own sentence never quite says is that the kiss landed: he that was called Judas, one of the twelve, went before them, and drew near unto Jesus to kiss him — and before that last verb can close, Christ's own question breaks in on it, Judas, betrayest thou the Son of man with a kiss? Ambrose does not read that interruption as a refusal: Our Lord kissed him, not that He would teach us to dissemble, but both that He might not seem to shrink from the traitor, and that He might the more move him by not denying him the offices of love — on that reading, the two heads pressed together here are exactly what happened, not a gesture broken off. Christ answers him by his own given name rather than any title he once held among the twelve; Chrysostom hears in it a name given rather like one lamenting and recalling him, than one provoked to anger, and finds the same gentleness in what Christ calls himself in the same breath, the Son of man, that is, the humble and meek, not Master, Lord or Benefactor. Past the raised staves and torches, high at the left, two unhaloed men watch from a gap in the rock, empty-handed: they which were about him saw what would follow, and said, Lord, shall we smite with the sword? — Luke's own question, though nothing on the wall says these two are the ones who asked it. Theophylact reads the moment as a contrast: the other disciples ask leave to strike, but Peter, always fervent in defense of his Master, waits not for permission. Lower on the wall, at the right, a grey-bearded elder in a golden mantle holds a small blade to the ear of a second man, whose head is bent down under his grip: Luke names neither of them, and gives the wound a side neither Matthew nor Mark does, his right ear — and the verse that follows the blow in Luke's own account, Suffer ye thus far. And he touched his ear, and healed him, has nothing painted here to answer to it; the fresco keeps the blade at his ear and leaves the mending to the words alone. Closer to the Lord, a young man already has both hands closed on his mantle while an armoured figure crowds in behind him — a seizing that, on Luke's own reckoning, belongs to the verse just past this passage's own close, Then took they him, and led him. What comes between is Christ's own words to the chief priests, and captains of the temple, and the elders, which were come to him, Be ye come out, as against a thief, with swords and staves? — a phrase Bede takes up as a difficulty in the same breath, since these same men are said to have stayed at the high priest's house and sent others to make the arrest; his answer is that they came in the persons of the men they sent, empowered to act in their name.",

"File:Judas's kiss (Monreale).jpg":
 "Byzantine craftsmen set this mosaic into Monreale cathedral in Sicily during the twelfth century, against a field of gold — the same panel Matthew's and Mark's readers already show, TRADITIO worked into the gold at the left of its band and DOMINI, cut away by this photograph's own edge, at the right — a Latin noun for what Judas is doing here. Christ's own face is set straight out of the panel under a cruciform halo, and Judas presses in from the right, pale-mantled, bringing his own face level with his — near enough that whether the kiss itself landed is a question this mosaic leaves exactly where Luke's own sentence leaves it: he that was called Judas, one of the twelve, went before them, and drew near unto Jesus to kiss him, before Jesus is already answering, Judas, betrayest thou the Son of man with a kiss? Luke's own text keeps the embrace and the actual laying on of hands as two separate acts, the second held back for the verse that opens the next passage; the mosaic does not wait that long — at the left of the crowd, the figure Mark's own reader already identifies as a beardless man has his arm reaching across to grip the fold of cloth at Christ's own collar, the seizing under way while Judas's own face is still at the Lord's cheek. Lower at the left, worn thin by eight centuries, an older man has one hand closed over the crown of a second man's head; no blade can be made out in the gold at this resolution, and the mosaic gives neither man a name. Luke gives the wound a side Matthew and Mark do not, his right ear — though not his alone among the four Gospels, since John gives it too; what is Luke's alone is the sequel, and he touched his ear, and healed him, which nothing in this mosaic paints. Overhead a lantern is fixed to a bent pole among a crowd of spearpoints, no flame visible in it at this resolution — a small dark shape against the gold that Luke's own closing line for this passage will set against a much larger dark, this is your hour, and the power of darkness. At the right edge one young man wears a peaked, pointed head-covering; Matthew's reader, describing what looks to be this same figure, calls it a pointed cap and says he has \"a pole in his raised hand,\" and that there is \"no cuirass on him and no armour anywhere in the panel\" — this photograph shows an open, raised hand near him, but cannot settle whose it is or what, if anything, it holds. Another shape rising just behind a second head, further left in the same crowd, may or may not be a second cap of the same kind, and this reading does not decide it either way. Luke's own naming of the chief priests, and captains of the temple, and the elders, which were come to him, raised a real difficulty for Bede in this same comment: these same men, he says, had stayed behind at the high priest's house and sent others to make the arrest, so that which were come to him means they came in the persons of the men they sent, empowered to act in their name — neither figure settles it either way.",

"File:Kiss of Judas (Church of Saint Nicolas the Orphan).jpg":
 "Thessaloniki's church of St Nicholas Orphanos keeps this fresco on one of its walls, painted in the fourteenth century among some of the finest surviving Palaiologan work anywhere in the city — the same wall Matthew's and Mark's readers already show. Judas presses in from the left, pale-robed, one hand already closed on the dark fold at the Lord's own breast, his own face brought level with his; Christ, at the right, is ringed in a halo of deep orange-red, and holds his own face angled just clear of the kiss. Luke's own verse for the moment never quite says the kiss was completed: he that was called Judas, one of the twelve, went before them, and drew near unto Jesus to kiss him — before Jesus is already speaking, Judas, betrayest thou the Son of man with a kiss?, using the name Judas was given at birth rather than any office he once held among the twelve. Off at the left edge, a robed figure has a long straight blade lifted overhead, striking nothing: of the three icons in this reader, this is the one where no blow is painted at all, at least in what this photograph keeps of the wall — no blade at anyone's head, no ear cut — so Luke's own sequel has nothing here to answer to either, and he touched his ear, and healed him. Beneath the dark fold of the Lord's own mantle, a closed white roll is just visible, gripped in a hand almost lost under Judas's own arm; it sits nearest, on this reading, to the words that close the passage — ye stretched forth no hands against me: but this is your hour, and the power of darkness — Luke's own alone, though the clause just before them, I was daily with you in the temple, is close to Mark's own wording of the same night. Above the whole crowd a lit lantern is lifted on its own pole with the monogram ΙΣ ΧΣ lettered beneath it, and every head pressed in under the raised spears and blades goes bare — no helmet, no mail anywhere on the wall, at least in what this photograph keeps of it. Chrysostom explains why a crowd like this one came by night at all: they had come at night fearing an outbreak of the multitude — the very darkness Luke's own closing line names.",

// ---- 22:54-62, the denial of Peter ----

"File:Alektor Petrou Dionysiou.jpg":
 "The same wall at Dionysiou that Matthew's and Mark's readers already show, Η ΑΡΝΗΣΙΣ ΤΟΥ ΠΕΤΡΟΥ lettered white across the night sky above it — the denial of Peter, its threefold refusal compressed onto one wall. Luke's own text names no porch and no going out before the very last verse of the passage, Peter went out, and wept bitterly; it is the Church's own harmonizers, reading Luke beside Mark and John, who have him leave the gate and return between the first denial and the second — Augustine works through the harmony in the Catena on this passage, that, as John says, he might again deny standing by the fire. The lower-left group — Peter seated among armed men and one unbearded, blue-clad young man, a hand lifted toward the fire — suits Luke's later two exchanges: another saw him, and said, Thou art also of them. And Peter said, Man, I am not; and, about the space of one hour after, another insisted he was a Galilaean, and Peter answered, Man, I know not what thou sayest. Neither challenger is named or given a face of his own in Luke's own account, and Luke's third answer carries no curse and no oath, where Matthew and Mark both have him begin to curse and to swear. At the door, a woman in red points at him — on this reading, Luke's own certain maid, though his own sentence keeps her exchange at the fire, not at any door: she beheld him as he sat by the fire, and earnestly looked upon him, and said, This man was also with him. Hers is the only one of the three he answers Woman, I know him not, a word none of the other three Gospels put in Peter's mouth here. Up at the right, over a green column, the third Peter has hidden his face in his own crossed arms; a red-combed cock stands beside him on a low pale block, its beak open. Immediately, while he yet spake, the cock crew — Luke gives it once, where Mark's own reader already notes that Mark has it crow twice. What no figure on this wall can give is the next clause: And the Lord turned, and looked upon Peter. There is no Christ anywhere in the fresco for that look to come from. Ambrose reads Peter's tears as the look's own evidence: the first and second time he denied and wept not, for as yet our Lord had not looked upon him. He denied the third time, Jesus looked upon him, and he wept bitterly — so that the bent figure over the column answers, on Luke's own account, a look the painter had no way to include.",

"File:Ravenna, sant'apollinare nuovo, int., storie cristologiche, epoca di teodorico 07.1 rinnegamento di pietro.jpg":
 "The same sixth-century mosaic from Sant'Apollinare Nuovo that Mark's reader already shows, and that John's reader shows again for John's own first denial — two figures only, ringed by a running band of blue shell-shapes, with no fire, no bystanders, no third scene and no cock. Luke sets his own first denial at the fire, not a door: a certain maid beheld him as he sat by the fire, and earnestly looked upon him, and said, This man was also with him — third person, said of him rather than to him, and answered all the same, Woman, I know him not, a word none of the other three Evangelists give Peter here. The doorway this mosaic paints belongs to John's own account, whose door has a keeper of its own (John 18:16); Matthew's and Mark's second denial instead sends Peter out to a porch. Luke's own text names no door at all, and no going out before its own closing verse, Peter went out, and wept bitterly — the leaving and returning the Church's harmonizers set between the first denial and the second comes from reading Luke beside Mark and John, not from Luke's own words. What the bare panel of a woman pointing and a hand raised in refusal cannot show is everything Luke adds after it: no fire, though his own text gives the first denial beside one; no third challenger, about the space of one hour after; and nothing at all of a line Luke alone gives, And the Lord turned, and looked upon Peter. Ambrose's comment on the denial draws exactly the contrast a scene with no Christ in it invites: Peter denied, because he promised rashly. He does not deny on the mount, nor in the temple, nor in his own house, but in the judgment-hall of the Jews. The hall is what this mosaic gives; the look Chrysostom's comment ties to Peter's tears is not something any picture of a doorway and a raised hand was ever going to include.",

// ---- 22:63-71, mocked and before the council ----

"File:Krinomenos Christou Dionysiou.jpg":
 "The same 1547 Dionysiou fresco Matthew's and Mark's readers already show, its title running the width of the dark sky above it: Ο ΧΣ ΚΡΙΝΟΜΕΝΟΣ ΥΠΟ ΑΝΝΑ ΚΑΙ ΚΑΙΑΦΑ, Christ judged by Annas and Caiaphas. Luke keeps neither name in this passage — no high priest is named anywhere in it — and in his whole Gospel the pair is named only once, years earlier and together: Annas and Caiaphas being the high priests, the word of God came unto John the son of Zacharias in the wilderness. Two elders share one throne at the left, its own tall back rising in a gold-edged arc behind them. The farther of the two from the prisoner is without hair, his beard run white, and has taken hold of his own collar with both hands, drawing the cloth open across his chest — a gesture belonging to Matthew's and Mark's own account of this night, when the high priest rent his clothes for the blasphemy he had just heard, and to no sentence of Luke's, who reports no such tearing anywhere in his own nine verses. Beside him, nearer the prisoner, the second elder holds one open hand raised before his own chest; no line of Luke's own gives either man a voice, since his account never lets one man question Christ alone in this scene, as Matthew's and Mark's high priest each does in theirs. Foremost of a small standing crowd between the throne and the prisoner — the elders of the people and the chief priests and the scribes, in Luke's own phrase for the assembly — one man reaches to a slanted writing-stand, a reed still raised in his other hand, an inkhorn and a written page already before him: testimony fixed even as it is heard, and Luke alone of the three Synoptics calls no witnesses in this passage at all, nothing of a clause Mark uses twice over the same false witness, that their witness agreed not together. The prisoner himself stands beneath his own halo, in blue over a tunic of deep red, both hands reaching toward that same writing-stand, his own profile turned away from the two elders, toward the far side of the scene — and in Luke's own nine verses he is given more to say than either Matthew or Mark allow him at this point in their own accounts: If I tell you, ye will not believe: and if I also ask you, ye will not answer me, nor let me go, a whole exchange that neither records. Bede reads plainly what lies behind the question that draws even this much out of him: They wished not for truth, but were contriving calumny. Because they expected that Christ would come only as man, of the root of David, they sought this of Him, that if He should say, I am the Christ, they might falsely accuse Him of claiming to Himself the kingly power. What the crowd beside him is never given, on Luke's own reckoning, is a single voice to speak through it: Matthew and Mark both put their one decisive question in the high priest's own mouth; Luke's own text asks it twice, first from the assembly together, Art thou the Christ? tell us, and only once he answers that, a second time, Art thou then the Son of God? — and Christ closes the two differently, the second on Ye say that I am, against Matthew's Thou hast said and Mark's bare I am. The one throne this wall paints belongs to the men judging him; the seat Luke's own Christ speaks of is not on this wall: Hereafter shall the Son of man sit on the right hand of the power of God. At the picture's own right border a soldier stands guard under a crested helmet; Luke's own text names no soldier among the elders of the people and the chief priests and the scribes who came together, as soon as it was day, and led him into their council.",

"File:Christ before Caiaphas, GRACANICA 1 090A7764.jpg":
 "The same wall Mark's reader already shows, set in Gračanica's own north aisle in Kosovo and dated to the fourteenth century, above a niche holding a stylite saint who belongs to a different subject in the church's own scheme entirely, not to this one. Both figures at the left show only the worn green underpaint where their own faces once were, and whatever ran along the top of the scene in paint is too faint here to read — nothing for this reading to test against Luke's own silence about a name, and the file's title, Christ before Caiaphas, is a modern cataloguer's word, not one legible on the wall itself. One of the pair stands; the other, nearer the doorway behind them, sits enthroned under a tall, rounded cap, a cushion of red beneath his feet — this copy of the fresco is too worn to support any claim about what his robe looks like beneath that point. A knot of elders fills the centre of the scene, one hand reaching from each side to meet in the gap between them — as close as paint comes to a pair of clauses Mark repeats almost word for word, that the witnesses could not be made to agree, testimony belonging entirely to Matthew's and Mark's own night session and to none of Luke's nine verses: no false witness is summoned anywhere in them, and the one place the word appears is where the council calls Christ's own answer witness enough on its own, What need we any further witness? for we ourselves have heard of his own mouth. Luke's own council puts its questions through no single high priest either, asking first as an assembly together and then, Then said they all, a second time, as the reading beside this one discusses at more length. At the right the prisoner advances on his own feet, a halo over him, a mantle of dark blue wrapped close, his head inclined a little; a soldier beside him wears a scaled collar and carries a hilt at his side, over a rose tunic marked with small crosses. In this passage Luke's own word for the men who hold him goes no further than that, the men that held Jesus. Sliced away at the very foot of the photograph, the top edge of a second scene on this same wall shows through: a cock on its column beside lettering in Slavonic that ends in Peter's own name. Mark's reader already reads the two scenes stacked in that order, trial above and denial below — Matthew and Mark both tell the trial before the denial closes, where Luke tells the denial first and the mocking after: an order of telling, not necessarily of the night's own events, as Augustine says in the Catena on this very passage: The temptation of Peter which took place between the mockings of our Lord is not related by all the Evangelists in the same order. For Matthew and Mark first mention those, then Peter's temptation; but Luke has first described the temptations of Peter, then the mockings of our Lord. Neither this wall nor the fresco beside it in this reader shows Christ blindfolded, though Luke opens his own passage with it: And when they had blindfolded him, they struck him on the face, and asked him, saying, Prophesy, who is it that smote thee? Mark's own Gospel keeps a plainer trace of the same act, to cover his face; what both walls paint instead is the scene Matthew and Mark set beside it, not a want of anything in Luke's own words.",


// ---- 23:1-5, before Pilate ----

"File:Pilate judgement (icon).jpg":
 "The same fifteenth-century Russian panel that Matthew's and Mark's readers already show, ПРИВЕДЕНИЕ КЪ ПИЛАТУ, the bringing to Pilate. Luke's own words for it: the whole multitude of them arose, and led him unto Pilate. Christ stands at the far left under his gold halo, in a mantle gone almost black with age, a grey-green-robed figure close at his shoulder and three green-crested helmets rising behind them both; Luke's own five verses here name no soldier at all. Pilate sits mid-panel, raised on a stepped dais, a wide gold band studded down its own length striping the green of his robe; his own face turns away from Christ toward the men beside him, while one hand is laid back over the throne behind him, on the prisoner's own side of it — a gesture Christ's own hands, kept at his waist, never answer. Whatever the red-robed figure leaning in behind the throne holds, Luke tells no washing — that detail is Matthew's own, the only Evangelist who gives Pilate water for his hands at all. Beside the throne a knot of men presses close, one in a plain white head-cloth, two more in red leaning shoulder to shoulder — counsellors or accusers, the panel does not say. Luke's own text presses on regardless, in words no picture carries: they began to accuse him, saying, We found this fellow perverting the nation, and forbidding to give tribute to Caesar, saying that he himself is Christ a King, a charge neither Matthew nor Mark spells out in words, though both say the accusing happened. Augustine's own comment on this verse says why that matters: \"Matthew and Mark do not give this, though affirming that they accused Him, but Luke has laid open the very charges which they falsely brought against Him.\" Bede calls the single answer Luke does give — Thou sayest it — \"the same words which He used to the Chief Priests,\" and reads in it already its own kind of verdict on the one who hears it: \"that Pilate might be condemned by his own voice.\" Neither Matthew's second question — Hearest thou not how many things they witness against thee? — nor Mark's — Answerest thou nothing? behold how many things they witness against thee? — nor the marvelling that follows either one has any place in Luke's own five verses: he tells no second question and no silence here, only Then said Pilate to the chief priests and to the people, I find no fault in this man. John's own Pilate says almost the same words, I find in him no fault at all — whether the two are one finding or two, this Catena block does not say. Luke's own account presses further still: they were the more fierce, saying, He stirreth up the people, teaching throughout all Jewry, beginning from Galilee to this place — Galilee named on purpose, Theophylact thinks, \"as desirous to alarm Pilate,\" for \"the Galileans were of a different sect and given to sedition,\" the detail that sends the case, two verses on, into Herod's own jurisdiction.",

"File:Cathedral (Monreale) - Right wing transept - Before Pilate.jpg":
 "The same twelfth-century mosaic in Monreale cathedral's right transept that Mark's and John's readers already show, its own Latin set into the gold ground below the top frieze, IHS DVCTVS E(ST) ANTE PYLATV(M), Jesus was led before Pilate — close to Luke's own words for the same moment, though the inscription's own passive voice names no one, where Luke's own verb puts the multitude itself in motion: the whole multitude of them arose, and led him unto Pilate. That multitude fills the mosaic's left half, row on row of elders in pale head-cloths pressed close together, the foremost raising a hand to his own mouth and, right at the front beside Christ, another with his hand lifted chest-high as if still arguing his point. Nothing on this wall can carry the words Luke gives this crowd: We found this fellow perverting the nation, and forbidding to give tribute to Caesar, saying that he himself is Christ a King. \"Most plainly are they opposed to the truth,\" Theophylact says, \"for our Lord was so far from forbidding to give tribute, that He commanded it to be given.\" Christ walks free of the crowd, cross-haloed, a dark mantle over him, his right hand reaching only as far as the doorway's own plain jamb; the space beyond it is left wholly unexplained on this wall, where John's own account gives it a reason Luke's does not, they themselves went not into the judgment hall, lest they should be defiled — a detail proper to John's own Passover, absent from Luke's five verses here. Pilate sits enthroned beyond the columns, PYLAT… lettered over his head, in a gold mantle, his own hand reaching only to the twisted column on his own side of the dark recess — a hand that never quite matches Christ's, each stopping on his own side of the gap between them: the gesture that stands for Luke's own single exchange, Art thou the King of the Jews? And he answered him and said, Thou sayest it, and nothing further — no second question put to him here, no charge of silence, none of the marvelling Matthew's and Mark's own Pilate does at this same point in their trials. Behind the throne a soldier in fish-scale mail keeps a bared blade upright at his own shoulder, a spear braced in his other hand — the only armed man on the whole wall; Luke's own five verses here name no soldier at all, and his next soldier, in the text that follows, is Herod's own men of war, and after them, at the cross itself, the soldiers also mocked him. The verdict that follows this one exchange in Luke's own next verse, Then said Pilate to the chief priests and to the people, I find no fault in this man, is words alone, nothing this mosaic sets out to picture. Luke's own account presses further still: they were the more fierce, saying, He stirreth up the people, teaching throughout all Jewry, beginning from Galilee to this place, naming Galilee, Theophylact thinks, on purpose, to alarm a Roman governor with a word for sedition — the same word that, two verses on, sends the prisoner into Herod's own jurisdiction.",

// ---- 23:33-43, the Crucifixion and the penitent malefactor ----

"File:Stavrosis Dionysiou.jpg":
 "The same 1547 fresco from Dionysiou that Matthew's and Mark's readers already show, spanning the plaster over two arches with its own title split down the middle by the cross's own upright, Η ΣΤΑΥΡΩΣΙΣ — Pilate's own charge is nowhere painted here, not even the three tongues Luke gives it in, Greek, and Latin, and Hebrew, which John's own Gospel gives too. Christ's own body hangs upright at the middle of the wall, ΙΣ ΧΣ lettered beside him, the head only a little bowed: he is dead in this picture, as he is in all four of Luke's icons here, where Luke's own text still has him speaking twice, first the prayer and last the promise. The malefactor at his right hangs with his head bowed low against his chest; the one at his left has his head turned in profile, chin down — the old convention still puts the man Bede, commenting on this very chapter, calls simply \"the thief on the right hand\" at Christ's own right, though Luke's eleven verses never say which side either man hung on, only that one railed and the other rebuked him. At each outer cross a soldier lifts a club against the roped legs: that maiming is John's own report, not this passage's, and nothing in Luke's own eleven verses puts a soldier this near to either man. Luke alone tells of a people who stood beholding; the rulers who derided him are in all three Synoptics, and Theophylact still reads no innocence into that beholding, since these are the same people \"who in truth had entreated that He should be crucified, waiting, namely, for an end.\" The wall paints neither the people nor the rulers, only the witnesses named below. A haloed woman, lettered ΜΡ ΘΥ, sinks against the shoulder of another in gold, with more behind her, and, on the far side, St John, lettered Ο ΘΕΟΛΟΓΟΣ, stands with one hand pressed to his jaw: the Mother and the beloved disciple together at the base of the cross are John's own picture. A helmeted soldier stationed beside the women lifts a spear toward Christ's ribs — that wound is John's alone too. Luke names no woman anywhere in these eleven verses; his own acquaintance and the women stand afar off only at 23:49, unnamed, and of the Twelve he names none here either. Beside Christ a young man in gold holds up a rod tipped with a sponge, a small pail at his feet — that belongs to the vinegar Matthew's, Mark's and John's own accounts give at the very end, which Luke does not repeat; the vinegar Luke's own soldiers already have in hand at 23:36 is fastened to no rod and no sponge of any kind. Past John stands a bearded, haloed figure in a red hood, a round shield strapped at his side and one hand lifted toward the cross: the centurion, who in Luke's next passage says Certainly this was a righteous man. Augustine, weighing that beside Matthew's and Mark's Son of God, finds no conflict between them: \"either we ought to understand that both these were said by the centurion, and that one Evangelist related one, another.\" Below the cross the ground itself opens, a skull set into the dark hollow — Athanasius, commenting on this passage's opening verse, gives the tradition the painter follows: \"which place the Jewish doctors say was the burial-place of Adam,\" so that Christ's own blood reaches down onto the skull of the first man. Under that again stand three more figures, one bare-chested with both arms raised — a detail this reader cannot confirm as Matthew's risen saints from this photograph alone, and Luke's own Gospel tells of no such rising in any case.",

"File:Crucifixion Icon Sinai 12th century.jpg":
 "A small twelfth-century panel from St Catherine's on Sinai, the same one Matthew's and Mark's readers already show, Christ alone on the cross with none of Luke's own crowd around him — no malefactor at either side, no soldier. Only IC XC is lettered on the short board above his head; none of Luke's own charge, in letters of Greek, and Latin, and Hebrew, THIS IS THE KING OF THE JEWS, is written anywhere on this panel. A pair of small angels rise against the gold ground just over the crossbar, their hands veiled — none of the four Gospels places an angel anywhere near this scene, and Luke's own pair wait until the empty tomb. Below, the Mother of God stands at the left with one hand lifted toward him, and St John, in green, sets his own cheek against his upturned palm at the right: their place together here is John's picture, not Luke's, whose own acquaintance and women stand afar off only at 23:49, unnamed, and whose own text names none of the Twelve anywhere near the cross. Where the cross stands, the rock opens into a small dark hollow with blood already running into it, though nothing shaped like a skull sits inside it, unlike the Dionysiou fresco of the same scene shown alongside it. A ring of sainted witnesses circles the whole board — apostles paired with archangels above, rows of bishops and martyrs down each edge, and a robed emperor and empress among monastics at the base — the panel's own devotional setting, no part of this chapter at all. What this small icon paints is the stillness after every word Luke gives Christ in these eleven verses has already been spoken: the prayer for his executioners, the promise to the one malefactor beside him — neither man, and neither sentence, has any place in the picture.",

"File:024 Crucifixion of Jesus Icon from Saint Paraskevi Church in Langadas.jpg":
 "St Paraskevi's own Greek icon at Langadas, dated 1855 at the lower right, the same panel Matthew's and Mark's readers already show. Christ hangs dead before a red brick backdrop with a tower rising at each corner, four letters set between points on the board above him, worn to little more than the shape of an abbreviation — closer to John's own fuller title, Jesus of Nazareth the King of the Jews, than to Luke's own six words, in letters of Greek, and Latin, and Hebrew, THIS IS THE KING OF THE JEWS. No malefactor hangs at either side of this cross, and its base is set not into split rock but into a mound of pale green stone, an arched hollow cut into it with a skull inside and two lines of blood already reaching it — Luke's own word for this place is Calvary, and he never calls it Golgotha, as Matthew, Mark and John all do. Three women, each haloed, are gathered shoulder to shoulder at the left — a presence Luke's own text does not tell of until 23:49, and even there leaves them unnamed and at a distance; and to the right John raises his eyes toward Christ, one hand pressed to his jaw, beside a bearded man in a scaled, gold-toned garment and a pale rounded cap — perhaps the centurion this passage does not yet quote, though nothing legible here marks him out for certain. Luke's own crowd at this cross — the people who stood watching, the rulers who derided him, the soldiers who mocked him and offered vinegar — is absent from this panel entire, and so are both malefactors and every word that passes between them and Christ.",

"File:Double-sided icon - Crucifixion and Hodegetria (9-13th c., Byzantine museum).jpg":
 "This is the earliest panel Athens' Byzantine Museum holds, the same processional icon Matthew's and Mark's readers already show, painted and repainted across three layers between the ninth and thirteenth centuries; the Virgin Hodegetria is on the reverse. Two archangels swoop down at the top corners with veiled hands, ΜΙΧΑΗΛ in red lettering at the left and ΓΑΒΡΙΗΛ at the right — no Gospel places an angel at this scene, and Luke's own pair wait until the tomb. Flanking the beam that crosses it the gold reads Ο ΒΑΣΙΛΕΥΣ ΤΗΣ ΔΟΞΗΣ, the King of Glory, a title of the Church's own confession rather than the charge Pilate had written, which Luke gives in three tongues, Greek, and Latin, and Hebrew — John's own Gospel gives the same three, and nothing of either survives on this small gold ground. Christ hangs dead, his head fallen to one side. From his side a fine red line runs down and out toward where his mother stands — a wound none of the three Synoptics tells, which only John's own lance explains. The Theotokos takes her place at his right, wrapped in a star-marked mantle, one hand curled near her throat and the other open beneath it; St John waits at his left, a palm cupping his own jaw — together they belong to John's Gospel, not Luke's, whose own acquaintance and women stand afar off only at 23:49, unnamed, with none of the Twelve named at all. Red stars are scattered thickly over the gold ground around them. Below their waists, John's own robe still falls in painted folds; Mary's side has worn to a dark, unreadable shape. Only the ground where the cross itself stands is left bare and gold, with no rock or skull visible now. No malefactor and no soldier appear anywhere on the panel, and none of Luke's own crowd — the people who stood watching, the rulers who derided, the soldiers who mocked and offered vinegar — has any place on so small and so spare a panel.",

// ---- 23:50-56, the burial ----

"File:Etesatou Ioseph Somatou Christou.jpg":
 "The opening scene of three Dionysiou frescoes on this burial, painted in 1547, its own title set across a night sky sown with stars, ΗΤΗΣΑΤΟ ΙΩΣΗΦ ΤΟ ΣΩΜΑ ΤΟΥ Ι(ΗΣΟ)Υ — Joseph asked for the body of Jesus, the same Greek verb Luke's own text gives at This man went unto Pilate, and begged the body of Jesus, rendered begged where Mark's own King James text has craved for the identical word. Pilate sits enthroned at the left, crowned and robed in red, one hand lifted; a young soldier stands at his shoulder in armour, a spear held aslant across him and a round shield behind his arm — a figure Luke's own seven verses give no place to, since he names no soldier anywhere near this asking. Joseph stands at the right, an elder with a grey beard and a halo, in a green mantle, his hands spread before him, empty and turned upward: a man asking, not one demanding. Luke's own account of him reaches back of the asking itself to say first what kind of man he was — a good man, and a just — and adds, in a parenthesis none of the other three Evangelists troubles to include, that he had not consented to the counsel and deed of them, meaning the very council that had only just condemned the Christ he now comes to beg for. Bede glosses the office Luke gives him, counsellor: \"A counselor, or decurio, is so called because he is of the order of the curia or council, and administers the office of the curia,\" and traces his city in turn, \"Arimathea is the same as Ramatha, the city of Helcanah and Samuel.\" What troubled Augustine about this scene is nowhere on the wall: how a man John calls a secret disciple, for fear of the Jews, could be the one man bold enough to ask Pilate for the body outright, when none of those who had followed Christ openly had dared to. His own answer sets the crowned throne and the armoured guard exactly where they belong in the account: \"We must understand then, that he did this from confidence in his rank, by which he might be privileged to enter familiarly into Pilate's presence.\" Of the verse's last clause, who also himself waited for the kingdom of God, the fresco paints nothing; it is a hope, not a posture, and no picture holds it.",

"File:Apokalthelosis Dionysiou.jpg":
 "The second of Dionysiou's three frescoes for this passage, its title Η ΑΠΟΚΑΘΗΛΩΣΙΣ, the unnailing, set either side of the cross's own upright, a small plaque fixed above it and the crossbeam running beneath, a pair of small angels entering at the corners and ΙΣ ΧΣ lettered close to the descending body. The fresco's own word for the scene is grander than Luke's: where the title names the unnailing outright, Luke's own seven verses give this half of the burial only the bare verb he took it down, with no tool named and no second man anywhere in the sentence. What the fresco supplies beyond that bare verb is the Church's own memory of how the work must have gone — an elder in a green mantle, unlettered but recognisable from the throne-room scene beside this one, gathering the body against himself as it comes free, while lower on the rock a kneeling man frees a nail with pincers, two already drawn and standing upright in a basket by his knee, and a ladder resting where it was left against the beam's edge. Around the body the Church has gathered mourners this passage does not name: the Mother of God pressing her face to her Son's as the body comes free; beside her a woman in gold holding his loose hand to her own cheek, with more women behind her in scarlet and blue; and, leaning in from the far side, a haloed figure with his own hand pressed to his own cheek — the place the Church gives its beloved disciple, though Luke names no disciple here at all — while at the edge another mourner in green stands apart, one hand raised to her brow. Luke's own witnesses do not embrace the body as these mourners do: his acquaintance and the women that followed him from Galilee had only stood afar off, beholding these things, at 23:49, and his next word of them, at 23:55, still has them only watch — followed after, and beheld the sepulchre, and how his body was laid. The plainest burial sentence among the four Gospels, and wrapped it in linen, carries none of Matthew's clean cloth, none of Mark's fine linen he had bought, none of John's hundred pounds of spice. Bede reads that bareness as a rebuke: \"By the simple burial of our Lord, the pride of the rich is condemned who not even in their graves can be without their wealth.\" St Ambrose turns it back toward whoever is reading: \"The just man covers the body of Christ with linen. Do you also clothe the body of Christ with His own glory, that you may be yourself just.\" Beneath it all the rock has split into a small dark hollow with a skull set inside, Adam's own grave under the cross by the Church's own tradition, the same one two of this reader's Crucifixion icons already show. Luke's own word for the place is Calvary; he never calls it Golgotha, as Matthew, Mark and John all do.",

"File:Epitaphios Threnos Dionysiou.jpg":
 "The last of Dionysiou's three frescoes for this passage, its own title Ο ΕΠΙΤΑΦΙΟΣ ΘΡΗΝΟΣ set either side of a bare cross two small angels attend, a small plaque fixed above the crossbeam, its lettering crisp at this resolution but not resolving into any word this reader can identify. The body is laid full length on pale cloth over a red stone bier, and no lament of any kind is in Luke's own seven verses: his text moves from the tomb's own hewing, by way of the preparation day and the sabbath drawing on, to the women who watched it, with not a word of weeping anywhere between. Bede takes the tomb itself, hewn in stone, wherein never man before was laid, as more than a detail — an argument: \"It is laid also in a new tomb, wherein never man before was laid, lest when the rest of the bodies remained after the resurrection, it might be suspected that some other had risen again.\" St Ambrose, without naming which Evangelist wrote which, finds it meaningful in itself that the Gospels do not all describe the tomb alike: \"Nor without meaning has one Evangelist spoken of a new tomb, another of the tomb of Joseph.\" Christ, he continues, is buried in a just man's tomb so that he may rest in the house of justice. Above the body's head the Mother of God, ΜΡ ΘΥ beside her, bows low until her face touches his; beside her, further along the body, a second haloed figure, ΙΩ lettered by his halo, has his own hand pressed to his own cheek as he bends low over the body; at the feet Joseph himself, the grey-bearded elder from the two icons beside this one, has both hands still in the cloth, folding it over — Luke's own he wrapped it in linen, painted at last; further off a woman in red throws one arm high, and, apart from her, a woman in a green veil holds a hand to her own mouth while another mourner in a red hood leans close beside her. Of the mourners, only Joseph is named by Luke; he names neither the Mother of God nor the beloved disciple bending over the body, and the women, though Luke does put women at this very scene, are here shown grieving in a way his own text withholds: the women also, which came with him from Galilee, followed after, and beheld the sepulchre, and how his body was laid — a watching, in his own words, not the lament the fresco paints. Away at the right a bearded man has a ladder braced against his own shoulder, one cheek set against his raised hand: the place Byzantine painting reserves for Nicodemus, a name Luke's own Gospel never once uses. What comes next in Luke is left unpainted here — they returned, and prepared spices and ointments; and rested the sabbath day according to the commandment — a labour Theophylact reads as proof of a faith still incomplete: \"for they had not yet sufficient faith, but prepared as if for a mere man spices and ointments, after the manner of the Jews, who performed such duties to their dead.\"",

// ---- 24:1-12, the myrrhbearing women ----

"File:Ide Topos pou Ekato Dionysiou.jpg":
 "The same Dionysiou fresco of 1547 that Matthew's and Mark's readers already show, its own caption running across the stars, ΙΔΕ Ο ΤΟΠΟΣ ΟΠΟΥ ΕΚΕΙΤΟ Ο Κ(ΥΡΙΟ)Σ — behold the place where the Lord lay. Neither half of that sentence is Luke's own: the form ἴδε ὁ τόπος is Mark's, from behold the place where they laid him, and where the Lord lay closes Matthew's own, come, see the place where the Lord lay. Luke gives his two men no such invitation at all; they answer the women's fear with a question first — why seek ye the living among the dead? — and only then, he is not here, but is risen. Two winged figures sit here: one on the tilted stone in front, a staff upright in his hand, his head turned back toward the women at the left even as his open hand reaches the other way, toward the rock; one further back on the rock beside the open coffin, a second staff upright in his own hand. St Ambrose asked outright why the four Gospels do not agree on the number: \"how is it that Mark has mentioned one young man sitting in white garments, and Matthew one, but John and Luke relate that there were seen two angels sitting in white garments.\" St Augustine, in the same block, works out the count rather than leaving it a disagreement: the women first saw \"an Angel sitting on the right hand, which Mark says,\" and only afterward, looking further in, \"they saw within two other Angels standing, (as Luke says,)\" who spoke to encourage them. The fresco settles nothing between them: both its angels sit, which is nearer Matthew's one seated angel than Luke's own two men, who stood by them, but there are two of them, which is Luke's own count and John's, not Matthew's one or Mark's one. Two women stand together at the edge of the scene. The one in green has a hand raised to her own chin; the one in dark red has her own hand raised too, resting near her companion's shoulder rather than her own face. Between them, not held by either alone, is a tall gold vessel — or a pair of them, stacked hand over hand; the resolution will not quite settle which. Luke's own opening verse already has certain others with them besides these two, and, later, once they have returned and told the eleven, he names three outright where the panel paints none: it was Mary Magdalene, and Joanna, and Mary the mother of James. Joanna is Luke's alone; no other Evangelist names her anywhere in the four Gospels. The great stone thrown down before the coffin answers to the plainest of the three Synoptic clauses for it — they found the stone rolled away from the sepulchre — without Mark's own added word on its size. The linen lying empty inside the coffin belongs, read against Luke's own text, not to this moment at all: his three verses here give the women nothing but the body's absence, and it is the passage's own last verse that gives the linen to Peter, coming after them — stooping down, he beheld the linen clothes laid by themselves — a visit this fresco has no figure to show. Down at the foot of the scene several soldiers in full mail and helmets are down or reeling: one pitched forward, head bowed near his own shield with a sword crossed on it, one arm flung wide; another gripping his own head. The watch is Matthew's alone to report — the keepers did shake, and became as dead men — for Luke's own twelve verses know no guard and post none. Disbelief meets the women's report in Mark's own longer ending too — they, when they had heard that he was alive, and had been seen of her, believed not — but the words idle tales, for what their report seemed to the eleven, are Luke's own alone: their words seemed to them as idle tales, and they believed them not.",

// ---- 24:36-43, peace be unto you ----

"File:Christos Apostolois Dionysiou.jpg":
 "Matthew's and Mark's readers both show this same Dionysiou fresco of 1547 as the type the Church keeps for the risen Lord standing among his own, and Luke's reader gives it the same standing rather than any scene from these eight verses. Worn, and only part of it legible, an epigram runs across the top rather than any Evangelist's own sentence — the Creator, having manifested the rising from the dead to those who were made by him — where Luke's own greeting for this meeting is shorter, and spoken in the text rather than lettered on the wall: peace be unto you. Christ is set upright between two companies of haloed men, both arms opened toward them, his feet on a red cushion, raised on a stepped footstool rather than any floor beneath him. Luke's own words for this moment name hands, feet, flesh and bone: behold my hands and my feet, that it is I myself: handle me, and see; for a spirit hath not flesh and bones, as ye see me have. Cyril of Alexandria and St Gregory the Dialogist both read that same showing as the showing of a wound: Cyril writes that Christ \"first shows them His hands and His feet, and the print of the nails\"; Gregory, on the very next clause, that \"he showed them his hands and his feet, on which indeed were clearly marked the prints of the nails.\" Neither painted palm is legible enough at this resolution to say whether the fresco follows them. No table stands anywhere in the fresco and nothing is set out to eat, though Luke alone among the Synoptics has the risen Christ ask for food and take it before them — a piece of a broiled fish, and of an honeycomb; and he took it, and did eat before them. Nor does any apostle in either rank draw back or shield his face, though the first word Luke gives this company at the sight of him is fright: they were terrified and affrighted, and supposed that they had seen a spirit. St Ambrose will not have Peter and John themselves doubt so plainly — \"we can not believe that Peter and John could have doubted\" — and reads the fear Luke reports as the larger company's own report covering the few. Bede reads the same fear more plainly: \"they do not believe that the real flesh could rise again from the grave on the third day. They think then that they see the spirit which He gave up at His passion.\" He names the error outright: \"This mistake of the Apostles was the heresy of the Manicheans.\" What the fresco does keep from these verses, without setting out to depict them, is the gesture under the greeting: two open arms answering, in shape if not in Luke's own words, to peace be unto you. St Gregory Nazianzen writes of that very word, \"peace is peculiarly of God, who binds all things together in one, to whom nothing so much belongs as the unity of nature, and a peaceful condition.\" The same open reach also anticipates a passage not painted here: a few verses on, Christ tells this same company that repentance and remission of sins should be preached in his name among all nations, beginning at Jerusalem.",

};
